{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,420p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/06_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_EXECUTION_CREW_PLAN.md && sed -n '1,180p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-033_EVIDENCE_DERIVED_CONFORMANCE.md && sed -n '1,180p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-034_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_AGENT_RUNTIME.md && sed -n '1,160p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-038_PRACTICAL_REPOSITORY_READ_SEARCH.md && sed -n '1,180p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-039_AGENT_RUNTIME_TASK_EXECUTION_SEPARATION.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,420p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/06_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_EXECUTION_CREW_PLAN.md && sed -n '1,180p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-033_EVIDENCE_DERIVED_CONFORMANCE.md && sed -n '1,180p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-034_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_AGENT_RUNTIME.md && sed -n '1,160p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-038_PRACTICAL_REPOSITORY_READ_SEARCH.md && sed -n '1,180p' Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-039_AGENT_RUNTIME_TASK_EXECUTION_SEPARATION.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Provider-Neutral Execution Crew Plan\r\n\r\n## Purpose\r\n\r\nThis document plans how the useful generic architecture demonstrated by the Assignment 3 AgentCrew can be extracted into the production `Pipeline/` without tying production execution to one model vendor.\r\n\r\n`AgentCrew/` remains preserved as historical Assignment 3 evidence. It is not the production runtime and will not be rewritten in place. Production work will extract its general orchestration lessons into new, provider-neutral components under `Pipeline/` after the current Phase 3 evidence work is complete.\r\n\r\nThis is a planning document, not architecture approval or runtime implementation.\r\n\r\n## Existing reusable strengths\r\n\r\nAssignment 3 demonstrated several ideas worth retaining:\r\n\r\n- independent, fresh agent invocations instead of one long shared model session;\r\n- explicit artifact handoffs between roles;\r\n- structured output schemas for planning and validation results;\r\n- role-specific tool restrictions, including read-only planning/validation and bounded write access for implementation;\r\n- separation between implementation and validation;\r\n- a bounded repair pass when validation reports concrete blockers;\r\n- explicit human Unity compilation, test, play-mode, and visual validation after static agent review.\r\n\r\nThe historical implementation also has limits that production extraction must correct:\r\n\r\n- the Claude CLI and its command-line permission vocabulary are embedded directly in the runner;\r\n- one global model is used for every role;\r\n- the implementation agent also authors the tests that judge its work;\r\n- validation is static and currently lacks a blocking test-isolation policy;\r\n- latest-output files are overwritten instead of being stored in immutable task/run directories;\r\n- the packager role exists for homework submission and is not part of normal game delivery.\r\n\r\nThe extraction should preserve the strengths without treating the historical implementation or its output claims as current production authority.\r\n\r\n## Provider-neutral rule\r\n\r\nThe following rule is non-negotiable:\r\n\r\n> No task contract, role prompt, output schema, evidence record, or deterministic validation gate may depend on a specific LLM provider.\r\n\r\nProvider-specific behavior belongs only behind provider adapters. Provider names, model identifiers, CLI flags, tool names, session identifiers, and API response shapes must not leak into task meaning or correctness policy.\r\n\r\n## Proposed production structure\r\n\r\nThe target layout is:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPipeline/\r\n  AgentRuntime/\n    agent_runner.py\r\n    contracts.py\r\n    providers/\r\n      base.py\r\n      claude_code.py\r\n      openai_codex.py\r\n    config/\n  TaskExecution/\n    contracts.py\n    task_runner.py\n  ExecutionCrew/\r\n    crew.py\r\n    roles/\r\n      implementer.md\r\n      unity_test_author.md\r\n      validator.md\r\n    schemas/\r\n  Testing/\r\n    run_unity_tests_clean.ps1\r\n\r\nDocs/Engineering/\r\n  UNITY_TESTING_POLICY.md\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is a target layout. This documentation task creates none of those runtime, provider, role, schema, test-runner, or policy files.\r\n\r\nThe intended separation is:\r\n\r\n- `AgentRuntime/` owns provider-neutral invocation contracts, budgets, adapter selection, and normalized results;\n- `TaskExecution/` owns NSC task identity and delegates its contained generic invocation to `AgentRuntime`;\n- `ExecutionCrew/` owns the bounded role sequence and role-specific schemas;\r\n- `Testing/` owns deterministic test execution rather than delegating test truth to an agent;\r\n- `Docs/Engineering/UNITY_TESTING_POLICY.md` will define the canonical isolation and evidence policy used by people, agents, and deterministic gates.\r\n\r\n## Provider-neutral contracts\r\n\r\n### Generic `AgentInvocationRequest`\n\r\nEvery provider consumes the same conceptual request. Its fields should cover:\r\n\r\n- `run_id`: immutable execution-run identity;\r\n- `role`: provider-neutral production role;\r\n- `prompt`: role instructions and bounded task instructions;\r\n- `context_paths`: explicit repository inputs available to the role;\r\n- `allowed_capabilities`: semantic capabilities such as read repository, search, edit allowed paths, or execute approved commands;\r\n- `write_boundaries`: allowed paths and prohibited paths, independent of provider permission syntax;\r\n- `output_schema`: provider-neutral structured-output contract;\r\n- `model_capability_class`: `low_cost`, `standard`, or `high_reasoning`, not a vendor model name;\r\n- `turn_budget`, `time_budget`, and `token_budget`: bounded execution limits, with unsupported measurements reported explicitly;\r\n- `provider_configuration_key`: reference to external configuration that selects an adapter and provider settings without changing the request's task meaning.\r\n\r\nThe request must not contain Claude-specific tool names, Codex-specific tools, or provider API payloads. Adapters translate the semantic capability and budget fields into the closest supported provider mechanisms and fail closed when a required restriction cannot be enforced.\n\nIt also must not contain NSC task identity. `TaskExecutionRequest` composes `schema_version`, `task_id`, `task_contract_identity`, and an exact `AgentInvocationRequest`. This keeps the dependency direction `ExecutionCrew -> TaskExecution -> AgentRuntime`; generic workflows such as ArchitectureReview and Reconciliation may call AgentRuntime directly.\n\r\n### Conceptual `AgentResult`\r\n\r\nEvery provider returns the same conceptual result. Its fields should cover:\r\n\r\n- `provider`: adapter/provider identity used for the invocation;\r\n- `model`: actual provider model identifier used;\r\n- `role`: requested production role;\r\n- `status`: normalized invocation outcome, distinct from delivery or conformance;\r\n- `structured_output`: schema-validated role output when available;\r\n- `changed_paths`: paths observed or reported as changed, subject to independent Git verification;\r\n- `duration`: measured wall-clock duration;\r\n- `usage`: token/cost/turn usage when the provider exposes it;\r\n- `raw_log_reference`: path to the immutable provider log, not embedded provider output;\r\n- `failure_classification`: normalized timeout, provider, permission, schema, budget, or execution failure;\r\n- `claims_execution_occurred`: whether the agent claims it ran commands or tests.\r\n\r\nAn agent's execution claim is only a claim. Deterministic tooling must independently establish whether commands ran, which files changed, and what Unity produced.\r\n\r\n## Provider adapters\r\n\r\n`ClaudeCodeProvider` and the future `OpenAICodexProvider` implement one provider interface. Both consume the same `AgentInvocationRequest` and return the same `AgentResult`.\n\r\nAdapters are responsible for:\r\n\r\n- translating allowed capabilities and write boundaries into provider tool permissions or sandbox controls;\r\n- delivering prompts and context through the provider's supported mechanism;\r\n- enforcing or validating structured output as strongly as the provider permits;\r\n- mapping provider-neutral capability classes to configured model names;\r\n- applying timeouts and reporting duration, turns, tokens, and cost when available;\r\n- preserving raw provider logs beneath the current immutable run directory;\r\n- normalizing provider-specific errors into common failure classifications.\r\n\r\nAdapters may translate:\r\n\r\n- allowed capabilities into provider tool permissions;\r\n- prompt and context delivery;\r\n- structured-output enforcement;\r\n- model names;\r\n- timeout and usage reporting.\r\n\r\nAdapters may not change task meaning, validation policy, or evidence authority. They may not weaken acceptance criteria, reinterpret write boundaries, turn an agent claim into test evidence, or declare a task ready, conformant, integrated, or complete.\r\n\r\n## Production roles\r\n\r\nThe initial production crew has three roles.\r\n\r\n### Implementer\r\n\r\nThe Implementer receives one already-selected bounded schema-v2 task contract and approved context. It changes production implementation only within explicit write boundaries, reports changed paths and blockers, and does not declare delivery success. It may repair implementation from concrete validation or runtime findings within a bounded GER budget.\r\n\r\n### Unity Test Author\r\n\r\nThe Unity Test Author independently translates the task contract's completion gates and relevant risks into tests. It is initially the `low_cost` proving role so the runtime can demonstrate role-specific model selection without placing low-cost reasoning on final semantic validation.\r\n\r\nIts normal write authority is limited to:\r\n\r\n- Unity test code;\r\n- test assembly definitions;\r\n- test artifacts produced or curated for the run.\r\n\r\nIt must not silently modify production code to make testing easier. If production testability requires a seam or design change, it returns a structured proposal to the Implementer. The Implementer then decides whether the bounded production change is valid under the task contract, after which tests and deterministic gates run again.\r\n\r\n### Validator\r\n\r\nThe Validator is read-only and uses a stronger model, normally `high_reasoning`. It performs semantic review against the task contract, approved canon/context, implementation diff, authored tests, and deterministic findings. Its result can request changes or identify ambiguity; it cannot establish test success, conformance, readiness, or delivery.\r\n\r\n### Planning-role change\r\n\r\nThe schema-v2 task contract replaces most of Assignment 3's per-run planning role. The production crew should not ask a fresh Planner to redefine already-approved scope on every run. If a selected contract is ambiguous or too broad, execution stops and returns a structured blocker for human review or later contract/decomposition work outside the crew.\r\n\r\nThe Assignment 3 packager has no normal production role. Phase 3 evidence tooling and the delivery workflow, not a homework packager, will eventually assemble committed delivery or revalidation records.\r\n\r\n## Model selection\r\n\r\nRole prompts name capability requirements, never vendor model names. Initial provider-neutral capability classes are:\r\n\r\n- `low_cost` for cheap, bounded proving work;\r\n- `standard` for ordinary implementation and repair;\r\n- `high_reasoning` for difficult semantic validation and high-risk reasoning.\r\n\r\nConfiguration maps a capability class plus a provider configuration key to a provider adapter and concrete model. This permits:\r\n\r\n- all-Claude crews;\r\n- all-OpenAI crews;\r\n- mixed-provider crews;\r\n- cross-provider validation experiments in which a different provider reviews implementation output.\r\n\r\nModel availability and exact names are operational configuration, not prompt or schema changes. Configuration must fail clearly when no approved model satisfies the requested capability class.\r\n\r\n## Deterministic authority boundary\r\n\r\nLLM output never establishes:\r\n\r\n- Unity test success;\r\n- a clean working tree;\r\n- integrated delivery;\r\n- current conformance;\r\n- readiness;\r\n- dispatch authorization.\r\n\r\nThose remain deterministic Git, Unity, and TaskGraph decisions. In particular:\r\n\r\n- a test-author or implementer claim does not replace the clean Unity test runner and its result artifacts;\r\n- changed-path claims do not replace Git diff and scope checks;\r\n- a Validator `pass` does not create a Phase 3 delivery, baseline, or revalidation record;\r\n- a committed evidence record is evaluated by TaskGraph logic against committed Git objects;\r\n- `taskcontrol ready` and `taskcontrol authorize` remain disabled until separately designed, proven, and approved.\r\n\r\n## Run artifacts\r\n\r\nEach execution uses an immutable task/run-scoped directory such as:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPipeline/ExecutionCrew/runs/<run-id>/\r\n  request.json\r\n  implementation_result.json\r\n  test_author_result.json\r\n  validation_result.json\r\n  provider_logs/\r\n  unity_results/\r\n  runtime_feedback/\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe run ID must be unique and immutable. A run directory is append-only after finalization; retries or repairs receive distinguishable attempt artifacts or a new run identity according to the later schema. Mutable convenience pointers may be considered separately, but they are never historical authority.\r\n\r\nThe directory preserves requests, normalized results, raw provider logs, deterministic Unity results, and human/runtime feedback. It does not by itself prove delivery.\r\n\r\nAfter integration and exact-commit validation, selected committed artifacts may be referenced by Phase 3 delivery, historical-adoption baseline, or revalidation records. Those evidence records bind the relevant artifact Git blobs, task-contract identity, canon identity, completion gates, integrated commit/tree, and required human approval. Final evidence should reference only artifacts that are appropriate to preserve, not blindly commit every raw provider transcript.\r\n\r\n## Relationship to GER\r\n\r\nThe responsibilities form this chain:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nExecution Crew produces bounded implementation/test/validation artifacts\r\n  -> deterministic execution produces actual Git/Unity evidence\r\n  -> GER may perform bounded repair from concrete findings\r\n  -> Phase 3 evaluates committed delivery/baseline/revalidation evidence\r\n  -> a human and deterministic policy decide subsequent action\r\n```\r\n\r\nGER may send concrete deterministic, Unity, runtime, or semantic findings back to the Implementer and, when appropriate, the Unity Test Author. Repair attempts have explicit budgets, no-op repair is failed progress, and exhaustion escalates. No worker, role, crew, adapter, or GER pass declares itself complete.\r\n\r\n## Relationship to TaskGraph\r\n\r\nTask selection remains human-approved for now. `taskcontrol ready` and `taskcontrol authorize` remain disabled.\r\n\r\nThe Execution Crew receives exactly one already-selected bounded task contract. It does not:\r\n\r\n- mutate `Tasks/*.yaml`;\r\n- select global priority;\r\n- derive or grant readiness;\r\n- authorize dispatch;\r\n- absorb unrelated prerequisites or redesign the task.\r\n\r\nAmbiguous scope, missing design, oversized work, or a substantial prerequisite becomes a structured blocker for human review or a future separately authorized contract/decomposition workflow.\r\n\r\n## Historical compatibility\r\n\r\nPreserve:\r\n\r\n- `AgentCrew/` as Assignment 3 historical evidence;\r\n- `Assignment6GER/` as Assignment 6 historical evidence.\r\n\r\nProduction extraction must not rewrite those directories to look as though they always used the new runtime. If later compatibility is useful, add adapters or wrappers that translate historical inputs/outputs at their boundary, or migrate callers to the new production interfaces. Historical schemas, logs, and behavior remain unchanged.\r\n\r\n## Staged implementation plan\r\n\r\nEach stage requires human review before its outputs become architecture or delivery authority.\r\n\r\n### Stage 0 - Documentation and architecture approval\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** Phase 3A architecture is understood; the Assignment 3 and Assignment 6 historical boundaries have been reviewed; this plan and ADR are available for human review.\r\n- **Output:** reviewed architecture comments and, only if the human accepts it, an approved or revised provider-neutral direction.\r\n- **Tests:** documentation link/path checks, requirement checklist review, and diff-scope review proving only authorized documentation changed.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no architecture-approval claim in this task; no runtime, adapter, crew, test runner, Unity, task-contract, or dispatch implementation.\r\n\r\n### Stage 1 - Canonical Unity testing policy and deterministic clean runner\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** Stage 0 direction is human-approved; Phase 3B remains the active milestone; existing Unity test commands and isolation risks are inventoried.\r\n- **Output:** `Docs/Engineering/UNITY_TESTING_POLICY.md`, `Pipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1`, and deterministic policy tests.\r\n- **Tests:** policy-schema/content checks; temporary-project or fixture checks for clean-copy/isolation behavior, exit codes, result/log preservation, and failure handling; an approved real Unity proving run when available.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no provider runtime, agent execution, role prompts, automatic evidence record, readiness, dispatch, or Unity gameplay changes.\r\n\r\n### Stage 2 - Complete NSC-023 Phase 3 baseline/revalidation proof\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** Stage 1's testing policy and runner are reviewed and passing; one exact integrated NSC-023 state is selected; required human Unity access is available.\r\n- **Output:** committed, exact-identity NSC-023 baseline evidence and a justified revalidation proof when a real relevant change exists or a controlled exercise is explicitly approved.\r\n- **Tests:** clean Unity gates; evidence-record schema validation; Git commit/tree, contract/canon hash, surface blob, artifact blob, ancestry, and current-conformance checks.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no fabricated gameplay change solely for revalidation; no readiness/dispatch enablement; no provider runtime extraction; no claim that other tasks are conformant.\r\n\r\n### Stage 3 - Extract provider-neutral `AgentRuntime`\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** Phase 3 is merged; Stage 2 has proven the evidence path; request/result semantics and authority boundaries are human-approved.\r\n- **Output:** provider-neutral contracts, runner interface, configuration loading, immutable run-layout support, and a fake provider for deterministic fixtures.\r\n- **Tests:** contract/schema fixtures; configuration validation; path/write-boundary rejection; budget/timeout normalization; immutable artifact behavior; fake-provider success and failure cases.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no live Claude or OpenAI invocation; no Execution Crew orchestration; no Unity changes; no dispatch authorization.\r\n\r\n### Stage 4 - Implement Claude and OpenAI adapters against the same fixtures\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** Stage 3 interface is stable; provider credentials/tools are configured outside contracts; approved provider-specific permission mappings are documented.\r\n- **Output:** `ClaudeCodeProvider` and `OpenAICodexProvider`, both conforming to the same request/result interface and shared fixture suite.\r\n- **Tests:** adapter contract tests; structured-output success/failure; permission and write-boundary translation; timeout, usage, raw-log, and failure normalization; opt-in live smoke tests with no production writes.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no provider-specific role prompts or task schemas; no change to validation/evidence policy; no automatic provider fallback that changes task meaning; no crew execution on a real task.\r\n\r\n### Stage 5 - Create provider-neutral ExecutionCrew roles\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** both adapters pass shared fixtures; canonical Unity testing policy is active; one selected schema-v2 fixture contract exists.\r\n- **Output:** provider-neutral Implementer, Unity Test Author, and Validator prompts/schemas plus bounded crew orchestration.\r\n- **Tests:** role schema tests; read/write-capability enforcement; test-author isolation tests; implementer-to-test-author proposal handoff; validator read-only checks; bounded-repair and circuit-breaker fixtures.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no global planning role; no homework packager; no autonomous task selection, task mutation, merge, evidence publication, readiness, or dispatch.\r\n\r\n### Stage 6 - Prove one task with a mixed or interchangeable provider configuration\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** Stage 5 fixtures pass; a human selects one safe bounded task and provider configuration; branch/worktree and rollback boundaries are explicit.\r\n- **Output:** one immutable run demonstrating either mixed providers or provider interchangeability for the same role contracts, with deterministic results preserved.\r\n- **Tests:** clean Git scope checks; clean Unity runner; shared schema validation; compare normalized results across provider configurations; human review of behavior and cost/quality findings.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no claim that provider outputs are identical; no broad benchmark conclusion from one task; no automatic merge, readiness, dispatch, or continuous execution.\r\n\r\n### Stage 7 - Connect execution outputs to GER and Phase 3 evidence\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** at least one real bounded crew run is reviewed; deterministic findings are normalized; the exact integrated validation workflow is established.\r\n- **Output:** bounded repair handoffs from concrete findings and a reviewed mapping from selected committed run artifacts to Phase 3 delivery/baseline/revalidation evidence.\r\n- **Tests:** failing-finding-to-repair fixtures; no-op repair detection; retry/cost/runtime circuit breaker; exact commit/tree and artifact blob checks; post-integration Unity revalidation where required; current-conformance regression.\r\n- **Non-goals:** no worker-authored completion truth; no automatic evidence authority from agent logs; no readiness/dispatch; no unbounded repair.\r\n\r\n### Stage 8 - Consider readiness/dispatch only after repeated successful real deliveries\r\n\r\n- **Entry criteria:** multiple real tasks have completed the selected-task, crew, deterministic validation, integration, and Phase 3 evidence path; failure and recovery behavior is documented; humans judge the process trustworthy enough to review dispatch policy.\r\n- **Output:** a separate proposal for evidence-derived dependency readiness and dispatch authorization, or an explicit decision to remain human-dispatched.\r\n- **Tests:** adversarial policy tests; stale/ambiguous/invalid evidence; dependency and exclusive-resource cases; dirty-tree and unintegrated-result rejection; budget/circuit-breaker and fail-closed behavior; human approval workflows.\r\n- **Non-goals:** this stage does not presume approval; no immediate continuous autonomy, parallel workers, automatic merge, or task-priority delegation.\r\n\r\n## Branch strategy\r\n\r\n- Current testing-safety and Phase 3 evidence work remains on `phase-3-evidence-derived-conformance`.\r\n- Provider runtime extraction begins only after Phase 3 is merged.\r\n- The proposed later implementation branch is `provider-neutral-execution-crew`.\r\n\r\nThis plan does not create, switch, merge, or approve either branch.\r\n\r\n## Immediate next slice\r\n\r\nThe immediate implementation after this planning commit is limited to:\r\n\r\n- `Docs/Engineering/UNITY_TESTING_POLICY.md`;\r\n- `Pipeline/Testing/run_unity_tests_clean.ps1`;\r\n- deterministic policy tests;\r\n- no provider runtime yet.\r\n\r\nPhase 3B remains the immediate milestone. The testing-safety slice supports its clean, exact-commit Unity evidence; it does not displace it.\r\n# ADR-033: Evidence-Derived Current Conformance\r\n\r\n- Status: Accepted\r\n- Date: 2026-08-22\r\n- Scope: Architecture Correction Phase 3A\r\n\r\n## Context\r\n\r\nSchema-v2 task contracts define approved work but deliberately contain no operational completion truth. A historical delivery can cease to conform when its contract, governing GDD, implementation surfaces, or evidence artifacts change. Working-copy files also cannot be repository authority because they are neither immutable nor integrated.\r\n\r\n## Decision\r\n\r\nIntroduce minimal immutable delivery, baseline, and revalidation records under `Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/<TASK-ID>/`. Evaluate current conformance exclusively from committed `HEAD` and referenced Git objects. A baseline establishes first trustworthy evidence for an implementation that predates the evidence system without claiming its original authorship or delivery date.\r\n\r\nEach record binds an exact semantic task-contract hash and revision, normalized canonical-GDD hash, validated commit/tree, conformance-surface blob set, exact completion-gate result set, committed evidence-artifact blobs, and any required human approval. Delivery records bind the validated state to the integrated state. Baseline records identify the actual integrated state tested without inventing delivery commits and can establish conformance exactly as delivery records can. Revalidation records form an acyclic, same-task ancestry chain from a delivery, baseline, or prior revalidation record.\r\n\r\nCurrent conformance is derived, never written. A later unrelated commit preserves conformance when all bound surfaces and canon remain unchanged. Contract changes require replanning; canon or tracked-surface changes require revalidation. Invalid or contradictory evidence fails closed.\r\n\r\nWhen several records remain valid, Git commit ancestry selects a unique strict descendant. Incomparable maximal records are ambiguous. `recorded_at` is audit metadata and never selection authority.\r\n\r\n## Consequences\r\n\r\n- Evaluation is repeatable for a given committed `HEAD` and does not depend on working-copy formatting or timestamps.\r\n- Evidence is auditable through native Git commits, trees, and blobs.\r\n- Record creation remains manual in Phase 3A; no production evidence record, including for NSC-023, is introduced.\r\n- Evidence-derived current-state inspection exists, but production delivery/baseline/revalidation evidence has not yet been proven on a real task.\r\n- Dependency-readiness and dispatch authorization policy remain outside this decision and are not enabled. Readiness is not derived, authorization remains denied, and zero tasks may be autonomously dispatched.\r\n- State inspection alone, including a `conformant` result, never authorizes execution.\r\n- Claiming, attempts, and supervision remain outside this decision and stay disabled.\r\n- GDDRAG and its index are unchanged; the evaluator reads the canonical GDD directly from Git.\r\n# ADR-034 - Provider-Neutral Agent Runtime\r\n\r\n## Status\r\n\r\nAccepted — 2026-08-22. Amended by ADR-039 on 2026-08-23.\n\r\n## Context\r\n\r\nAssignment 3 demonstrated useful execution-crew patterns through fresh agent invocations, artifact handoffs, structured schemas, role-specific permissions, implementation/validation separation, bounded repair, and required human Unity validation. Its runner is also tied directly to the Claude CLI, applies one global model to every role, gives test authorship to the Implementer, relies on static model validation, and overwrites latest outputs.\r\n\r\nAssignment 6 reused parts of that runner and proved the broader GER lesson: concrete evaluator and human Unity findings can drive bounded repair, no-op repair is failed progress, and a worker's static pass is not completion evidence.\r\n\r\nProduction execution must support both Claude Code and OpenAI/Codex without duplicating task contracts, role semantics, validation policy, or evidence formats. This work must not interrupt Architecture Correction Phase 3B, which remains the immediate milestone.\r\n\r\n## Decision\r\n\r\nThe proposed production architecture adopts these rules:\r\n\r\n1. Production agent roles, invocation request/result contracts, prompts, and schemas are provider-neutral.\n2. Claude Code and OpenAI/Codex are interchangeable provider adapters behind one production `AgentRuntime` interface.\r\n3. Both adapters consume the same generic `AgentInvocationRequest` and return the same conceptual `AgentResult`.\n4. Provider adapters may translate permissions, context delivery, structured-output mechanisms, model names, timeouts, usage, logs, and provider failures. They may not change task meaning, validation policy, or evidence authority.\r\n5. Model selection uses provider-neutral capability classes configured outside role prompts.\r\n6. Historical coursework directories remain preserved: `AgentCrew/` is Assignment 3 evidence and `Assignment6GER/` is Assignment 6 evidence. Later compatibility uses wrappers or adapters rather than rewriting history.\r\n7. Important correctness invariants are enforced deterministically rather than delegated to model intelligence. LLM output cannot establish Unity test success, a clean working tree, integrated delivery, current conformance, readiness, or dispatch authorization.\r\n8. Implementation is staged. The canonical Unity testing policy and clean deterministic runner come first; provider runtime extraction begins only after Phase 3 is merged and does not interrupt Phase 3B.\r\n\r\nThe detailed staged plan and target layout are recorded in `Docs/AI-Pipeline/06_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_EXECUTION_CREW_PLAN.md`.\n\nADR-039 corrects an implementation mistake in the initial Stage 3 contract: NSC task identity is not part of generic provider invocation. `TaskExecution` owns task identity and depends downward on `AgentRuntime`; generic workflows may invoke AgentRuntime directly.\n\r\n## Consequences\r\n\r\nPositive consequences:\r\n\r\n- task contracts and evidence remain stable when providers or models change;\r\n- all-Claude, all-OpenAI, mixed-provider, and cross-provider validation configurations can use the same role semantics;\r\n- provider permission and response differences are isolated and testable;\r\n- deterministic Git, Unity, and TaskGraph mechanisms retain correctness authority;\r\n- historical course evidence remains truthful and auditable.\r\n\r\nCosts and constraints:\r\n\r\n- adapters require shared conformance fixtures and fail-closed capability mapping;\r\n- provider feature differences must be normalized without pretending their behavior is identical;\r\n- raw provider logs and normalized results require immutable run-scoped storage;\r\n- role isolation, especially independent test authorship, adds orchestration steps;\r\n- no runtime implementation may be treated as approved merely because this ADR is proposed.\r\n\r\n## Deferred decisions\r\n\r\nThis ADR does not approve:\r\n\r\n- concrete Python contract fields or JSON schemas;\r\n- provider SDK/CLI choices or exact model mappings;\r\n- automatic provider fallback;\r\n- production role prompt wording;\r\n- readiness, dispatch, task claiming, merging, or continuous autonomy;\r\n- changes to Phase 3 evidence authority.\r\n\r\nThose details require staged implementation, fixtures, real-task evidence, and human review.\r\n# ADR-038 — Practical Repository Read/Search\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted — 2026-08-23.\n\n## Context\n\nNo Safe Circle is developed in a trusted, single-user local environment. The developer, checked-out repository, Docker environment, and Claude account are trusted. The immediate priority is useful repository-aware agents that can help build the game, not a hostile multi-tenant filesystem-security platform.\n\n## Decision\n\nStage 4B.2 accepts Claude Code's native `Read`, `Glob`, and `Grep` tools for inspecting the actual No Safe Circle working tree. `/workspace` is the intended repository working area in Docker.\n\n`repository_read` exposes `Read`. `repository_search` exposes `Glob` and `Grep`. Requests with both expose all three. Empty-capability requests retain the fresh temporary workspace and no-tool behavior from Stage 4B.\n\nRepository-relative `context_paths` and the generated instruction to inspect only No Safe Circle files under `/workspace` are task guidance, not hardened sandbox enforcement. The project explicitly accepts the limited risk that native read/search tools may technically read elsewhere in the container. Claude is instructed not to inspect `/home`, credentials, environment secrets, or unrelated filesystem locations.\n\nRepository writing remains unsupported. Shell and approved command execution remain unsupported. Web access remains unsupported. `Bash`, `Edit`, `Write`, `NotebookEdit`, `WebSearch`, and `WebFetch` remain explicitly disabled.\n\n## Consequences\n\nAgents can inspect current code, documentation, Tasks, tests, Unity assets, pipeline files, and uncommitted development changes without repository copying, snapshots, brokers, MCP, or custom search infrastructure.\n\nStronger containment may be revisited if this pipeline is later used in an untrusted, shared, or production environment. For the current trusted project, work should proceed toward useful Execution Crew and game-development capability rather than further repository-security research.\n# ADR-039 — AgentRuntime / TaskExecution Separation\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted — 2026-08-23.\n\n## Context\n\nAgentRuntime was intended to be the provider-neutral layer for one bounded AI invocation. Its initial `AgentRequest` accidentally required an `NSC-###` task ID, `TaskContractIdentity`, and a matching `Tasks/<task_id>.yaml` path. Generic workflows such as ArchitectureReview, Reconciliation, architecture synthesis, and GDD audits could not honestly use that contract without pretending to be game-task execution.\n\nThis correction is made before broader production adoption so the pipeline does not accumulate workflow-specific AgentRunner entry points or fake task identities. Stage 4B.2 practical Claude repository read/search remains complete and unchanged.\n\n## Decision\n\nAgentRuntime owns generic bounded invocation only. Its immutable `AgentInvocationRequest` contains `schema_version`, `run_id`, `role`, `prompt`, `context_paths`, `allowed_capabilities`, `write_boundaries`, `output_schema`, `model_capability_class`, `budgets`, and `provider_configuration_key`. AgentRuntime owns provider selection, invocation, structured output, normalized failures, usage, logs, and immutable generic artifacts. It has no knowledge of NSC task naming or `Tasks/*.yaml`.\n\n`Pipeline/TaskExecution` owns `TaskContractIdentity` and immutable `TaskExecutionRequest`, composed from `schema_version`, `task_id`, `task_contract_identity`, and an exact `AgentInvocationRequest`. `TaskExecutionRunner` publishes a deterministic immutable task-level request artifact and delegates the exact invocation to `AgentRunner`, which publishes a task-neutral `request.json`.\n\nThe dependency direction is:\n\n```text\nExecutionCrew -> TaskExecution -> AgentRuntime -> provider adapter\ngeneric workflow -------------> AgentRuntime -> provider adapter\n```\n\nAgentRuntime never imports TaskExecution. ExecutionCrew must use TaskExecution rather than bypass task semantics. Higher-level generic workflows may use AgentRuntime directly. Providers remain interchangeable behind AgentRuntime.\n\n## Consequences\n\nTask identity remains auditable without contaminating generic provider requests. TaskExecution artifacts do not establish delivery, conformance, readiness, authorization, approval, integration, or completion. Historical AgentRuntime artifacts remain unchanged historical truth and are not reinterpreted as the new request type.\n\nArchitectureReview is not migrated in this slice. No OpenAI/Codex provider, ExecutionCrew orchestration, repository-write support, command execution, provider fallback, TaskGraph authority, Unity execution, GER integration, or Git automation is added. The next useful proof is migrating ArchitectureReview through generic AgentRuntime using Claude.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"wc -l Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md AgentCrew/README_Assignment3.md 2>/dev/null; rg -n -i 'fail|wrong|runtime|human|no.op|repair|camera|mutation|scene|test|validator|overwrite|stale|timeout|error|blocked' Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md AgentCrew --glob '*.md' --glob '*.json' --glob '\"'!outputs/**'\"' | sed -n '1,320p'; sed -n '1,420p' Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"wc -l Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md AgentCrew/README_Assignment3.md 2>/dev/null; rg -n -i 'fail|wrong|runtime|human|no.op|repair|camera|mutation|scene|test|validator|overwrite|stale|timeout|error|blocked' Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md AgentCrew --glob '*.md' --glob '*.json' --glob '\"'!outputs/**'\"' | sed -n '1,320p'; sed -n '1,420p' Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md\"","aggregated_output":" 245 Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md\n 245 total\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:16:- Assignment 6 adds the **GER orchestration**, a game-specific evaluator contract, runtime-feedback handling, and a **Circuit Breaker**.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:28:> **Fixed Isometric Camera and Projection Setup**\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:30:The goal was to replace the disposable perspective test camera with the fixed orthographic isometric presentation required by the GDD.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:36:For this run, the camera implementation had to satisfy these requirements:\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:38:1. The gameplay Main Camera uses **orthographic projection**.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:41:4. The implementation stays inside the selected camera/projection task instead of expanding into unrelated systems such as Tilemaps, spells, enemies, or sprite sorting.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:60:   PASS       FAIL\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:62:Unity Test   Assignment 5 Implementation Agent\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:68:        repeated failures\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:72:           Human Review\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:75:The Circuit Breaker allows a maximum of **three refinement attempts**. If the system cannot self-correct within that limit, the task is escalated for human review.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:81:The Assignment 5 implementation agent generated the first camera implementation.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:85:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:86:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs`\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:88:The implementation changed the camera to orthographic and added tests.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:96:- no free camera rotation,\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:99:However, the checked-in Unity scene was still stale, so I rebuilt the Door Prototype scene in Unity and tested the result manually.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:101:### Unity Runtime Test 1 — Failure\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:103:The camera was orthographic, but the game was not usable:\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:105:- the camera faced the wrong direction;\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:106:- the camera did not follow the player;\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:107:- the static evaluator had interpreted “fixed camera” too narrowly.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:111:`Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback000.json`\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:113:The pipeline was updated so human Unity runtime failures could re-enter the GER loop as evaluation feedback.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:121:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/IsometricCameraFollow.cs`\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:123:and modified the scene builder and camera tests.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:125:The camera could now translate with the player while preserving a fixed isometric orientation.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:131:I rebuilt and tested the scene in Unity again.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:133:### Unity Runtime Test 2 — Failure\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:135:The camera still did not frame the game correctly. The player-follow behavior existed, but the viewing side and framing were wrong and the starting door was no longer properly visible.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:139:`Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback001.json`\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:141:Runtime feedback files are preserved as an append-only history:\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:144:runtime_feedback000.json\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:145:runtime_feedback001.json\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:146:runtime_feedback002.json\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:156:For Assignment 6, I changed that behavior. A no-op refinement is now treated as a **failed repair attempt**, not a pipeline error.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:158:The GER progress guard detected the no-op and forced the loop to continue instead of accepting a false success.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:162:The final allowed refinement modified the scene builder and its tests again. The Evaluator verified the current camera implementation and the additional regression safeguards.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:168:- camera translation/follow without free rotation,\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:169:- stable camera-to-player offset behavior,\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:170:- tests for camera framing and fixed rotation,\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:175:At this point, all **three allowed refinements** had been used. If the feature had failed again, the Circuit Breaker would have escalated the task to human review.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:179:After the final GER pass, I rebuilt the Door Prototype scene in Unity and tested the result manually.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:181:The camera now behaves as intended: it uses a **Diablo / Ultima Online-style orthographic isometric presentation**, follows the player appropriately, and does not allow free camera rotation.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:183:I then ran the relevant Unity automated tests:\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:185:- the full `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests` EditMode suite;\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:186:- the existing `DoorInteractionPlayModeTests` PlayMode suite.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:188:**All tests passed.**\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:196:The first camera implementation passed static evaluation because it was orthographic and used a fixed isometric rotation. In Unity, however, it faced the wrong direction and did not follow the player.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:200:Human Play Mode testing exposed those failures. The failures were converted into structured runtime feedback and fed back into GER so the Refiner could continue working on the same selected goal.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:202:The pipeline also caught a different failure during Pass 3: the Refiner made no code changes even though unresolved runtime feedback existed. A GER progress guard rejected that no-op repair and counted it toward the Circuit Breaker instead of allowing the pipeline to fail silently.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:225:runtime_feedback000.json\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:226:runtime_feedback001.json\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:234:This history makes it possible to reconstruct what each agent did, what failed, what feedback was provided, and how the implementation changed.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:242:- **Refiner:** repeatedly used evaluator and Unity runtime feedback to repair the implementation.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:243:- **Circuit Breaker:** bounded the loop to three refinement attempts and would have escalated to human review if the final attempt failed.\r\nAssignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md:245:The selected camera feature ultimately passed both the GER Evaluator and real Unity validation.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:23:All gameplay, Editor, and test files must remain beneath:\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:41:- Separate interaction state from raw keyboard polling so tests can drive it deterministically.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:46:- Do not directly edit a Unity scene file.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:48:## Scene Builder\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:52:`No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene`\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:56:`Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity`\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:58:The generated scene should contain:\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:63:- A camera\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:66:- A simple damage-test control or documented way to trigger damage\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:70:## Testing\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:72:Create Play Mode tests that verify at least:\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:88:- Scene-generation instructions\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md:89:- Test instructions\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:14:- Every implementation, Editor, assembly-definition, and test file under:\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:33:- Public or internal test seams exist so tests do not require fake keyboard input.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:34:- Play Mode tests cover all required cases.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:37:- Runtime scripts do not reference `UnityEditor`.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:38:- The scene builder creates and wires a complete prototype.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:39:- The scene builder can run repeatedly.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:40:- No direct Unity scene YAML was authored.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:42:- No obvious C# compiler errors or missing references are present.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:48:Do not fail merely because Unity has not yet been executed inside this Docker container. Human Unity compilation and Play Mode execution are documented later.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:50:Return `\"needs_changes\"` when there is a specific code, contract, test, reference, or compilation problem that the implementation agent can fix.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md:52:Every failed criterion must name the relevant file and required correction.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/01_planner.md:29:- Require an Editor scene-builder command.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/01_planner.md:30:- Require Play Mode tests.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:1:# Human Playtest Feedback — Door Progress Indicator\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:17:## Required Repair\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:19:Inspect the generated UI component, scene-builder configuration, and door progress binding.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:23:Ensure the scene-builder command produces the correct configuration every time it runs.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:27:## Human Retest Requirements\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:29:1. Rebuild the prototype scene.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md:35:7. Run the scene-builder twice and confirm the repair persists.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:33:- Interaction state is testable without simulated keyboard input.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:38:- A Unity Editor menu command must build the prototype scene so manual Inspector wiring is minimized.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:39:- The scene builder must be safe to run more than once.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:40:- Play Mode tests must cover completion, movement cancellation, damage cancellation, and interaction release.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:57:- Runtime generative AI\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:60:## Human Responsibilities\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:62:The human developer will:\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:66:- Run the scene-builder menu command.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:67:- Inspect the generated scene.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md:68:- Run the Play Mode tests.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:22:- [x] `Docs/architecture.mmd` shows all four agents, artifacts, the pass/fail gate, the repair loop, and the human verification step.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:25:- [x] `README.md` names the game, explains crew output, game connection, agent roles/inputs/outputs, pipeline command, output locations, scene-builder and Play Mode instructions, human verification steps, known limitations, and AI usage statement.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:27:## Successful Unity Compilation *(human step — pending)*\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:28:- [ ] Open project in Unity `6000.1.8f1`, confirm scripts compile with zero console errors.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:30:## Successful Play Mode Tests *(human step — pending)*\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:31:- [ ] Run `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.DoorInteractionPlayModeTests` in the Test Runner; confirm all five tests pass.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:33:## Playable Prototype *(human step — pending)*\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:34:- [ ] Run **No Safe Circle â†’ Build Door Prototype Scene**.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:35:- [ ] Play the scene: confirm prompt visibility, progress fill, release/movement/damage cancellation, and post-completion doorway passability.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:37:## GitHub Repository *(human step — pending)*\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md:40:## Final Submission Review *(human step — pending)*\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:6:human-approved presentation enhancement. This does not reopen or reinterpret\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:13:`DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildControlsHud`. It is a background `Image` with\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:21:[Debug/Test] K - Take Damage\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:36:  Key.K;` and is explicitly commented as a testing/demo affordance, not a\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:37:  combat system. The HUD shows this binding prefixed with `[Debug/Test]` so it\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:40:  in the scene.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:44:- **No new runtime script.** The HUD is static, build-time-authored UI with\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:58:  already in `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.KnownRootNames` and is fully\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:65:  progress-bar track; the label uses the same `LegacyRuntime.ttf` builtin\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:66:  font already used elsewhere in the scene. No new assets or packages.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:70:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs` —\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:72:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs` —\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:73:  added three Edit Mode tests: HUD exists and is a distinct object from the\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:78:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef` —\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:79:  added a reference to `Unity.InputSystem` so the new test can read the\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:82:No runtime gameplay files were changed; door timing, cancellation, and\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:85:### Human Retest Steps\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:88:2. Run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene**.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:96:   repair below is unaffected).\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:104:10. Run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene** again; confirm no\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:105:    Console errors and exactly one `ControlsHud` panel (no duplicates), then\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:107:11. Open **Window → General → Test Runner** and run the **Edit Mode** tests\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:108:    in `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor` and the **Play Mode** tests\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:109:    in `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests`; confirm all pass.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:111:## Targeted Repair — Progress Indicator Not Visually Updating (2026-08-05)\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:113:### Human-Reported Defect\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:115:From `AgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md`: the door opens correctly after\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:121:The runtime binding in `DoorInteractionUI.Update()` (`progressFillImage.fillAmount\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:123:already correct. The defect was in scene generation: `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildUI`\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:133:surviving a scene rebuild.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:135:### Repair\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:137:`Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`:\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:147:- No runtime script changes were needed — `DoorInteractionUI` and\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:149:  generated scene configuration, so rebuilding the scene is sufficient and no\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:154:New `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/` Edit Mode test assembly\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:155:(`NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef`, Editor-platform only,\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:156:referencing the runtime and Editor assemblies) with\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:157:`DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs`:\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:159:  scene builder and asserts the generated `Canvas/ProgressFill/Fill` `Image`\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:162:  would have failed against the pre-repair builder and fails again if the\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:168:The existing five Play Mode tests in\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:169:`Tests/DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs` (completion, half-duration progress,\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:172:already correct and is unaffected by this repair.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:174:### Exact Human Retest Steps\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:177:2. Run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene**.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:187:8. Exit Play Mode and run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene**\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:188:   again; confirm no Console errors, no duplicated Hierarchy objects, and\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:189:   repeat steps 3–7 to confirm the repair persists across rebuilds.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:190:9. Open **Window → General → Test Runner**: run the **Edit Mode** tests in\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:191:   `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor` and the **Play Mode** tests in\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:192:   `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests`; confirm all pass.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:196:The Sealed Door Prototype described in `AgentCrew/inputs/door_feature_brief.md` and approved in `AgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json`: a placeholder WASD player approaches a sealed door, holds E for a configurable, default-5-second uninterrupted duration to open it, with deterministic cancellation on release, movement, or damage, backed by an in-range prompt and a progress-fill UI element, and generated entirely through an idempotent Editor scene-builder command.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:200:Runtime (`Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/`):\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:201:- `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.asmdef` — runtime assembly, references `Unity.InputSystem` and `UnityEngine.UI`.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:202:- `Scripts/DoorInteractable.cs` — owns `Duration`, `Progress` (0–1), `IsOpen`, `IsInteracting`, `IsPlayerInRange`; exposes `StartInteraction()`, `CancelInteraction()`, and a public `Tick(float deltaTime)` used both by its own `Update()` and directly by tests for deterministic time advancement. Owns the door's trigger-zone detection (`OnTriggerEnter`/`OnTriggerExit`) and notifies `PlayerInteractionController`.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:203:- `Scripts/PlayerInteractionController.cs` — the sole gateway between input/physics and the door. Exposes `BeginInteraction()`, `EndInteraction()`, `OnPlayerMoved()`, `NotifyDoorInRange()`, `NotifyDoorOutOfRange()`. Reads `Keyboard.current` (new Input System) in `Update()` only to call the same public methods tests use directly. Subscribes to `PlayerHealth.Damaged` to cancel on damage.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:207:- `Scripts/DebugDamageControl.cs` — clearly-labeled testing/demo affordance: pressing K or clicking the \"DEBUG: Take Damage\" button calls `PlayerHealth.TakeDamage`. Explicitly documented as not a combat system.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:210:- `Editor/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Editor.asmdef` — Editor-only assembly referencing the runtime assembly.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:211:- `Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs` — `No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene` menu command. Opens the existing `DoorPrototype.unity` if present (or creates a new empty scene), destroys any existing root objects matching a fixed set of known names, then rebuilds the entire hierarchy from scratch and saves. All cross-object serialized references (door visual/blocker, UI bindings, controller/health links, debug button listener) are wired via `SerializedObject` / direct property assignment, not manual Inspector work. Also builds `Canvas/ControlsHud`, a static always-visible controls panel (see the Scope Amendment section above).\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:213:Tests:\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:214:- `Tests/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.asmdef` — Play Mode test assembly referencing the runtime assembly, `UnityEngine.TestRunner`, `UnityEditor.TestRunner`, and NUnit.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:215:- `Tests/DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs` — five `[UnityTest]` cases (see Test Instructions below).\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:216:- `Tests/Editor/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef` — Editor-platform-only Edit Mode test assembly, added during the progress-indicator repair (see below), referencing the runtime and Editor assemblies, and `Unity.InputSystem` (added for the Controls HUD tests).\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:217:- `Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs` — Edit Mode tests that run the scene builder and assert its generated UI configuration: progress-fill sprite/idempotency (progress-indicator repair) and Controls HUD presence/text/idempotency (Controls HUD scope amendment).\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:219:Not created by this agent: `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity`. Per the contract, this file must only be produced by running the menu command inside the Unity Editor and is intentionally not hand-authored.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:223:- **Time advancement is decoupled from frame rate.** `DoorInteractable.Tick(float deltaTime)` is public specifically so Play Mode tests can advance the 5-second timer deterministically (e.g., 100 × 0.05s steps) instead of waiting on real wall-clock time via coroutines.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:225:- **`PlayerInteractionController` is the only class that touches `Keyboard.current`.** All state transitions (`BeginInteraction`, `EndInteraction`, `OnPlayerMoved`) are public methods independent of input polling, satisfying AC-6; the Play Mode tests never touch the Input System.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:227:- **The debug damage control is a single small script** (`DebugDamageControl`) wired to both a keypress (K) and a UI button via `UnityEventTools.AddPersistentListener`, kept intentionally minimal and explicitly commented as a testing/demo affordance only.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:228:- **Scene-builder idempotency** is achieved by destroying a fixed list of known root GameObject names before every rebuild rather than trying to patch an existing hierarchy in place — simpler to reason about and guarantees no duplicates or stale references across repeated runs.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:230:## Scene-Generation Instructions\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:233:2. In the menu bar, run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene**.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:234:3. The command creates/replaces `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity` containing: a floor plane, two flanking wall cubes forming a doorway gap, a door root (trigger zone + visual/blocking door cube), a player capsule with `CharacterController`, a directional light, a camera, a Canvas with an interaction prompt, a progress-fill image, a top-left Controls HUD panel, an `EventSystem` using `InputSystemUIInputModule`, and a labeled \"DEBUG: Take Damage (K)\" button.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:235:4. Re-running the command from any state (empty project, already-built scene, or a manually edited copy) is safe — it will rebuild the known objects fresh and leave everything else in the scene untouched.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:237:## Test Instructions\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:239:1. Open **Window → General → Test Runner**, switch to the **Play Mode** tab.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:240:2. Run `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.DoorInteractionPlayModeTests`. It does not require the generated scene — each test builds its own minimal `DoorInteractable`/`PlayerInteractionController`/`PlayerHealth` GameObjects.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:248:- Scene visual/interaction correctness (AC-7, AC-8, AC-10, AC-11, AC-12) requires manual verification in the Unity Editor, which this agent cannot perform.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md:252:No enemy AI, NavMesh, ranged enemies, Frost Field, Force Wave, Fireball, door locking/durability/breaking, multiple rooms, custom art/imported assets, runtime generative AI, or Unity MCP integration were implemented. `Packages/manifest.json` and `ProjectSettings/` were not modified — no external packages were added.\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:15:- Relevant implementation and test files\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:45:- Unity scene-builder instructions\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:46:- Play Mode test instructions\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:47:- Human verification steps\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:59:- Unity scripts and tests\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:63:- A pass/fail validation gate\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:64:- Repair loop from failed validation to implementation\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:67:- Human Unity compilation, testing, and final approval\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:82:- Remaining human Unity checks\r\nAgentCrew/prompts/04_packager.md:95:- Successful Play Mode tests\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:6:- **Pipeline result:** Success — validation passed on the first implementation attempt (no repair pass required). See `AgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json`.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:12:   - **Output:** `AgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json` — 13 acceptance criteria, approved/out-of-scope lists, required files, dependencies, risks, and test cases.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:16:   - **Output:** Runtime scripts, Editor scene-builder, Play Mode tests, and asmdefs under `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/`; `AgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md`.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:23:   - **Input:** `assignment_requirements.md`, `door_feature_brief.md`, `feature_contract.json`, `implementation_summary.md`, `validation_report.json`, `crew_run_log.json`, implementation/test files\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:28:`AgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json` reports `\"status\": \"pass\"` with zero blocking issues. All 13 acceptance criteria (AC-1 through AC-13) and 4 technical checks (asmdef structure, Editor-code confinement, no hand-authored scene YAML, no obvious compile errors) pass static review. Remaining items (AC-7, AC-8, AC-10, AC-11, AC-12) explicitly require a human to run the scene-builder and Play Mode tests inside the Unity Editor — this is documented as expected, not a defect.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:48:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:49:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.asmdef`\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:50:- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs`\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:51:- (Produced only by a human running the Editor menu command, not by this crew: `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity`)\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:55:`AgentCrew/orchestrator.py` runs each agent as an independent `claude -p` invocation with a restricted tool set and a structured JSON schema (for the planner and validator). Each agent's only shared state with the next is a file artifact on disk:\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:59:- The orchestrator checks that `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/` and `implementation_summary.md` exist before invoking the validator.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:60:- The validator's structured output is written to `validation_report.json`; the orchestrator branches on `status`. On `\"needs_changes\"`, it re-runs the implementer with the validation report as required reading (repair pass), then re-validates — up to one repair pass is permitted before the orchestrator raises an error and halts.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:67:- **Remove the Validation Agent:** unverified code would flow straight to packaging with no static check for compile risk, missing acceptance criteria, or excluded systems; the pass/fail gate that governs the repair loop and packaging invocation would not exist.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:70:## Remaining Human Unity Checks\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:72:1. Open the project in Unity `6000.1.8f1` and confirm the generated scripts compile without errors.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:73:2. Run **No Safe Circle â†’ Build Door Prototype Scene** and inspect the generated hierarchy and wired references; run it a second time and confirm no duplication.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:74:3. Run the five Play Mode tests in the Test Runner and confirm all pass.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md:75:4. Play the scene manually: verify the prompt, progress fill, all three cancellation paths (release/movement/damage), and that the doorway is passable after completion.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:3:  \"summary\": \"Static review of the Controls HUD scope amendment and the prior progress-indicator repair finds no blocking code, contract, test, reference, or compilation problems. Labels are hardcoded to match the current default key bindings (E, K, WASD), the HUD is rebuilt fresh every run alongside the rest of Canvas with dedicated idempotency tests, it is a sibling object positioned to avoid the prompt/progress bar, the progress-fill sprite regression fix is intact and covered by an Edit Mode test, no runtime script touches UnityEditor, and no excluded systems or external packages were introduced. Human Play Mode verification of visual layout is still required and cannot be substituted by this review.\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:6:      \"id\": \"HUD-1: scene builder creates a readable controls panel every time it runs\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:8:      \"evidence\": \"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:269 calls BuildControlsHud(canvasObject.transform) at the end of every BuildUI() call, and Canvas is in KnownRootNames (line 27) so it is destroyed and rebuilt from scratch on every Build(). Covered by Build_ControlsHud_ExistsSeparateFromPromptAndProgressIndicator in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:44-55.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:13:      \"evidence\": \"PlayerInteractionController.cs:9 defaults interactKey=Key.E, DebugDamageControl.cs:11 defaults damageKey=Key.K, PlayerMovement.cs:28-31 reads WASD — all match the hardcoded HUD text 'WASD - Move / Hold E - Open Door / ... K - Take Damage' in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:305-309. Build_ControlsHud_TextMatchesActualImplementedControls (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:58-76) asserts the actual serialized damageKey value's ToString() appears in the HUD text.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:18:      \"evidence\": \"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:305-309 text literal contains all three; asserted by StringAssert.Contains checks at DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:64-67.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:21:      \"id\": \"HUD-4: debug-only controls omitted or clearly labeled Debug/Test\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:23:      \"evidence\": \"HUD text line is '[Debug/Test] K - Take Damage' (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:309); DebugDamageControl.cs:6-7 doc-comments it as a testing/demo affordance; test asserts 'Debug' substring present (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:74-75).\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:28:      \"evidence\": \"ControlsHud is a sibling of InteractPrompt/ProgressFill under Canvas, anchored top-left (0,1)/(16,-16) sized 300x110 (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:277-284), while InteractPrompt is anchored (0.5,0.2) and ProgressFill at (0.5,0.12) — non-overlapping by construction. Test Build_ControlsHud_ExistsSeparateFromPromptAndProgressIndicator confirms distinct objects. Actual rendered layout still requires human Play Mode confirmation.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:31:      \"id\": \"HUD-6: repeated scene builds do not duplicate HUD\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:33:      \"evidence\": \"Canvas root is fully destroyed and rebuilt every run (ClearExistingObjects, DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:78-87), and Build_RunTwice_DoesNotDuplicateControlsHud (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:79-92) asserts exactly one ControlsHud child after two consecutive builds.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:38:      \"evidence\": \"DoorInteractable.cs (Tick/StartInteraction/CancelInteraction/Complete) and PlayerInteractionController.cs are unchanged by the HUD addition per the implementation summary; DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs still covers completion, half-duration progress, release/movement/damage cancellation with no modifications noted.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:43:      \"evidence\": \"HUD background/text use AssetDatabase.GetBuiltinExtraResource<Sprite>('UI/Skin/Background.psd') and Resources.GetBuiltinResource<Font>('LegacyRuntime.ttf'), both already used elsewhere in the builder; Packages/manifest.json contains only the pre-existing dependency set with no new external packages; grep of the DoorPrototype tree found no enemy/NavMesh/ability/door-lock systems (NavMesh* matches in DoorPrototype.unity are standard Unity scene boilerplate fields, not a navigation system).\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:48:      \"evidence\": \"Three new Edit Mode tests added in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs (HUD existence/separation, text-matches-implementation, no-duplication-on-rerun); Tests/Editor asmdef updated to reference Unity.InputSystem so the test can read the actual serialized damageKey.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:51:      \"id\": \"Progress-indicator repair preserved\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:53:      \"evidence\": \"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:220-235 assigns AssetDatabase.GetBuiltinExtraResource<Sprite>('UI/Skin/UISprite.psd') to the Fill Image (fixing the null-sprite defect described in human_playtest_feedback.md), and Build_ProgressFillImage_HasSpriteAssignedSoFillAmountIsVisible (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:12-24) regresses on it. DoorInteractionUI.cs:21-24 binding of fillAmount = door.Progress is unchanged.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:58:      \"evidence\": \"Runtime asmdef has no includePlatforms restriction and no UnityEditor reference; Editor asmdef is Editor-only and references the runtime assembly; Tests.Editor asmdef is Editor-only, references both runtime and Editor assemblies plus Unity.InputSystem for the new SerializedObject-based key check; grep confirmed no runtime script under Scripts/ references UnityEditor.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:61:      \"id\": \"No direct scene YAML authoring\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:63:      \"evidence\": \"All hierarchy/UI construction for the HUD and prior fixes is done via C# GameObject/Component calls in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs; DoorPrototype.unity on disk is a generated artifact (contains ControlsHud/DebugDamageButton object names matching the builder's output), not hand-edited.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:69:    \"HUD control labels for movement (WASD) and interact key (E) are hardcoded string literals, not read from the actual serialized fields at build time — only the debug damage key label is test-verified against the real serialized value (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:71-73). If interactKey or the movement scheme is ever changed via Inspector or code, the HUD text would silently go stale with no test to catch it. Not blocking since current defaults match, but worth tightening if those fields become configurable in practice.\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:70:    \"Human Play Mode verification is still required to confirm the HUD is actually legible, correctly positioned, and non-overlapping at runtime — this review only confirms non-overlap by anchor/size arithmetic, not rendered layout.\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:71:    \"The checked-in DoorPrototype.unity appears to be a byproduct of a prior scene-builder execution (likely from running the Edit Mode tests, which call DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build() and save to the real scene path); this is expected given the architecture but the human developer should still do a final clean rebuild-and-inspect pass as instructed.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:78:    \"AgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:81:    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:88:    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:89:    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:92:    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.asmdef\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:93:    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/validation_report.json:94:    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:19:      \"result_excerpt\": \"{\\\"acceptance_criteria\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-1\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Holding the interact input while stationary, in range, and undamaged for the full configured duration (default 5.0s) opens the door and permits crossing the doorway.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: drive the interaction start method on the interaction component, advance simulated time by 5+ seconds without cancelling, assert IsOpen becomes true and progress reaches 1.0.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-2\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Door opening progress is exposed as a public normalized float (0 to 1) that increases monotonically while an uninterrupted interaction is held.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance time to 2 of 5 seconds, assert progress is approximately 0.4 within tolerance of Â±0.05.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-3\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Releasing the interact input before completion cancels the attempt and resets progress to exactly 0.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, call the public release/end method, assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-4\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Player movement during an active, incomplete interaction cancels the attempt and resets progress to exactly 0.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, invoke the public movement-cancellation entry point directly (not via WASD polling), assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-5\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Player damage during an active, incomplete interaction cancels the attempt and resets progress to exactly 0.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, call the health component's public TakeDamage method, assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-6\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Interaction state (start, cancel, progress, completion) is driven entirely through public C# methods/properties that do not require simulated keyboard or Input System device events, so tests can drive it deterministically.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Code review of the Play Mode test fi\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:30:      \"result_excerpt\": \"All required files (except the Editor-generated `.unity` scene, which per contract must be produced by running the menu command in Unity) are in place.\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nImplemented the Sealed Door Prototype under `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/`:\\n\\n- **Runtime**: `DoorInteractable` (progress/duration/open state + public `Tick()` for deterministic testing), `PlayerInteractionController` (input-independent `BeginInteraction`/`EndInteraction`/`OnPlayerMoved`/range notifications), `PlayerMovement` (WASD via new Input System), `PlayerHealth` (`TakeDamage` + event), `DoorInteractionUI` (prompt/progress binding), `DebugDamageControl` (labeled testing affordance, key + button).\\n- **Editor**: `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs` implementing `No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene`, idempotently rebuilding the full scene with all references auto-wired via `SerializedObject`.\\n- **Tests**: 5 Play Mode tests covering completion, half-duration progress, release/movement/damage cancellation, all driving state through public methods only (no simulated input).\\n- **Docs**: `AgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md` written with design decisions, scene-build/test instructions, limitations, and confirmation no excluded systems were added.\\n\\nNo packages, ProjectSettings, or scene YAML were touched directly. Next step is for a human to open the project in Unity, run the scene-builder menu command, and execute the Play Mode tests — I could not do this myself since I don't have access to the Unity Editor.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:34:      \"prompt\": \"AgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:41:      \"result_excerpt\": \"{\\\"blocking_issues\\\":[],\\\"compile_risks\\\":[\\\"None identified from static review; standard Unity Test Framework asmdef pattern (UnityEditor.TestRunner referenced with no platform restriction) matches Unity's default template and should compile normally.\\\",\\\"UnityEditor.Events.UnityEventTools and UnityEditor.SceneManagement are used in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs without an explicit 'UnityEditor' asmdef reference entry, but these are part of the core UnityEditor assembly which is implicitly available to Editor-platform-restricted asmdefs, so this is expected to compile.\\\"],\\\"criteria_results\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DoorInteractable.StartInteraction()/Tick(deltaTime) accumulate deltaTime/duration into Progress and call Complete() at >=1; PlayerInteractionController.BeginInteraction() gates on IsInRange/IsOpen before calling StartInteraction. Covered by Completion_OpensDoor_AfterFullDuration test (DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs:37-47).\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-2\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DoorInteractable.Progress is a public get-only float (DoorInteractable.cs:15) updated monotonically in Tick(). Progress_IsApproximatelyHalf_AtHalfDuration test asserts ~0.5 at half duration. Note: contract's acceptance_criteria text for AC-2 describes checking ~0.4 at 2s, while the contract's own test_cases section describes checking ~0.5 at 2.5s (half duration) — these two contract sections are mutually inconsistent; the implementer followed the test_cases version. This is a contract authoring inconsistency, not an implementation defect, and does not block AC-2's substantive requirement (monotonic normalized progress).\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-3\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"PlayerInteractionController.EndInteraction() calls CurrentDoor.CancelInteraction(), which sets IsInteracting=false and Progress=0f exactly. Covered by ReleasingInteraction_CancelsAttempt test.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-4\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"PlayerInteractionController.OnPlayerMoved() calls EndInteraction() when IsInteracting. Cover\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:52:      \"result_excerpt\": \"All four required outputs are complete since the validation gate passed (`status: \\\"pass\\\"`, zero blocking issues):\\n\\n- **`README.md`** — game/assignment overview, agent roles table with explicit inputs/outputs, why each role is necessary, pipeline command, output locations, scene-builder/Play Mode/human verification instructions, known limitations, AI usage statement.\\n- **`Docs/architecture.mmd`** — Mermaid flowchart covering the brief â†’ planner â†’ contract â†’ implementer â†’ scripts/tests â†’ summary â†’ validator â†’ report â†’ pass/fail gate (with repair loop back to implementer) â†’ packager â†’ docs â†’ human approval.\\n- **`AgentCrew/outputs/run_report.md`** — run timeline, per-agent inputs/outputs, validation result, files produced, coordination mechanism, why each agent is non-removable, and remaining human checks.\\n- **`AgentCrew/outputs/submission_checklist.md`** — concise checklist distinguishing completed crew/documentation items from pending human Unity steps (compilation, Play Mode tests, playtesting, repo commit, final review).\\n\\nNo gameplay code was modified. Nothing was committed or pushed — that remains a human step per the pending checklist items.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:55:      \"role\": \"Door and Interaction Agent — Human Playtest Repair\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:63:      \"result_excerpt\": \"The repair is complete. Summary of the diagnosis and fix:\\n\\n**Root cause**: The runtime code (`DoorInteractable.Tick`, `DoorInteractionUI.Update`) was already correct — `progressFillImage.fillAmount = door.Progress` runs every frame. The bug was in `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildUI`: the progress `Image` was created with `type = Filled` but no `sprite` assigned. Unity's `Image.OnPopulateMesh` silently ignores `fillAmount`/`fillMethod`/`type` and draws a static full rect whenever `sprite == null` — confirmed directly in the checked-in scene YAML (`m_Sprite: {fileID: 0}`, `m_Type: 3`).\\n\\n**Fix** (`Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`): split the progress indicator into a background track and a child \\\"Fill\\\" image, and assigned the built-in `UI/Skin/UISprite.psd` sprite (via `AssetDatabase.GetBuiltinExtraResource`, no external asset) to the fill image so `fillAmount` now has real geometry to animate.\\n\\n**New automated coverage**: added an Edit Mode test assembly (`Tests/Editor/`) with tests that run the scene builder and assert the fill image has a non-null sprite with correct `Type`/`FillMethod`, plus an idempotency check that rebuilding twice doesn't duplicate the UI element.\\n\\n`AgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md` now documents the reported defect, diagnosis, fix, new test coverage, and exact human retest steps. Since I can't run the Unity Editor here, the existing on-disk `DoorPrototype.unity` still reflects the old (buggy) config until you rerun **No Safe Circle â†’ Build Door Prototype Scene**.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:66:      \"role\": \"Unity Validation Agent — Human Repair Review 1\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:67:      \"prompt\": \"AgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:74:      \"result_excerpt\": \"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Static validation of the targeted progress-indicator repair finds a correctly diagnosed and implemented fix with matching regression coverage. All required repair criteria have concrete static evidence. No blocking code, contract, test, reference, or compilation issues were found. Human Play Mode retest (per the documented steps, including re-running the scene builder) remains required before final sign-off, since the currently committed DoorPrototype.unity on disk still reflects the pre-repair UI configuration.\\\",\\\"criteria_results\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R1-ProgressUI-ConfiguredByBuilder\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs BuildUI() creates a 'ProgressFill' background Image and a nested 'Fill' child Image, assigning UI/Skin/UISprite.psd as the sprite with type=Filled, fillMethod=Horizontal, fillOrigin=Left. This directly fixes the diagnosed root cause (a Filled Image with sprite==null always renders full/solid regardless of fillAmount). New Edit Mode test Build_ProgressFillImage_HasSpriteAssignedSoFillAmountIsVisible in Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs asserts sprite non-null, type Filled, fillMethod Horizontal after Build().\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R2-UI-ReceivesProgressContinuously\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Scripts/DoorInteractionUI.cs Update() runs progressFillImage.fillAmount = door.Progress every frame, unconditionally while progressFillImage is assigned; DoorInteractable.Tick (called from its own Update) mutates Progress every frame during an active interaction, so with a real sprite now assigned the fill will visibly animate continuously.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R3-Reset-OnRelease-Movement-Damage\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DoorInteractable.CancelInteraction() sets Progress = 0f. PlayerInteractionController.EndInteraction() (called from EndInteraction on release, OnPlayerMoved, and HandleDamaged on PlayerHealth.Damaged) invokes CurrentDoor.CancelInteraction(). DoorInteractionUI.Update() re-reads \"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:85:      \"result_excerpt\": \"The summary reads correctly. Work is complete:\\n\\n**Summary of changes:**\\n- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs` — added `BuildControlsHud`, generating a compact top-left panel under `Canvas` (sibling of `InteractPrompt`/`ProgressFill`) showing `WASD — Move`, `Hold E — Open Door`, the interruption rule, and the debug damage key explicitly labeled `[Debug/Test] K`. Labels were verified against the actual code (`PlayerMovement`, `PlayerInteractionController.interactKey`, `DebugDamageControl.damageKey`), not assumed.\\n- Idempotency comes for free: `Canvas` is already destroyed/rebuilt wholesale on every run, so the HUD can't duplicate — no new guard logic needed.\\n- `Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs` — added tests asserting the HUD exists as a distinct object, its text matches the real movement/interact/debug key bindings, and rebuilding twice doesn't duplicate it.\\n- `Tests/Editor/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef` — added `Unity.InputSystem` reference needed by the new test.\\n- `AgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md` — updated with the scope amendment, design decisions, files changed, and human retest steps.\\n\\nNo runtime door/interaction/progress code was touched, and the prior progress-indicator repair is preserved. Human verification in the Unity Editor (Play Mode + Test Runner) is still required to confirm visually.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:89:      \"prompt\": \"AgentCrew/prompts/03_validator.md\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:96:      \"result_excerpt\": \"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Static review of the Controls HUD scope amendment and the prior progress-indicator repair finds no blocking code, contract, test, reference, or compilation problems. Labels are hardcoded to match the current default key bindings (E, K, WASD), the HUD is rebuilt fresh every run alongside the rest of Canvas with dedicated idempotency tests, it is a sibling object positioned to avoid the prompt/progress bar, the progress-fill sprite regression fix is intact and covered by an Edit Mode test, no runtime script touches UnityEditor, and no excluded systems or external packages were introduced. Human Play Mode verification of visual layout is still required and cannot be substituted by this review.\\\",\\\"criteria_results\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"HUD-1: scene builder creates a readable controls panel every time it runs\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:269 calls BuildControlsHud(canvasObject.transform) at the end of every BuildUI() call, and Canvas is in KnownRootNames (line 27) so it is destroyed and rebuilt from scratch on every Build(). Covered by Build_ControlsHud_ExistsSeparateFromPromptAndProgressIndicator in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:44-55.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"HUD-2: displayed labels match the actual input implementation\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"PlayerInteractionController.cs:9 defaults interactKey=Key.E, DebugDamageControl.cs:11 defaults damageKey=Key.K, PlayerMovement.cs:28-31 reads WASD — all match the hardcoded HUD text 'WASD - Move / Hold E - Open Door / ... K - Take Damage' in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:305-309. Build_ControlsHud_TextMatchesActualImplementedControls (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:58-76) asserts the actual serialized damageKey value's ToString() appears in the HUD text.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"HUD-3: panel includes WASD movement, Hold E to open, and interruption rule\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:305-309 text literal contains all three; asserted by StringAssert.Contains checks at DoorPrototypeSc\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:99:  \"human_repair_status\": \"validation_passed\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:100:  \"human_repair_started_at_utc\": \"2026-08-05T00:15:18.281422+00:00\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:101:  \"human_playtest_feedback_file\": \"AgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json:102:  \"human_repair_finished_at_utc\": \"2026-08-05T00:20:22.590294+00:00\",\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/approved_scope_amendment_controls_hud.md:1:# Human-Approved Scope Amendment — Controls HUD\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/approved_scope_amendment_controls_hud.md:13:The human developer approves this small presentation enhancement without\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/approved_scope_amendment_controls_hud.md:30:test key is shown, label it clearly as a debug/test control and use the actual\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/approved_scope_amendment_controls_hud.md:40:- The scene-builder command must create and wire the panel every time it runs.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/approved_scope_amendment_controls_hud.md:41:- Running the scene builder repeatedly must not duplicate the HUD.\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/approved_scope_amendment_controls_hud.md:44:## Human Acceptance Criteria\r\nAgentCrew/inputs/approved_scope_amendment_controls_hud.md:50:5. Rebuilding the scene twice does not create duplicate panels.\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:6:      \"verification\": \"Play Mode test: drive the interaction start method on the interaction component, advance simulated time by 5+ seconds without cancelling, assert IsOpen becomes true and progress reaches 1.0.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:11:      \"verification\": \"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance time to 2 of 5 seconds, assert progress is approximately 0.4 within tolerance of ±0.05.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:16:      \"verification\": \"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, call the public release/end method, assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:21:      \"verification\": \"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, invoke the public movement-cancellation entry point directly (not via WASD polling), assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:26:      \"verification\": \"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, call the health component's public TakeDamage method, assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:30:      \"requirement\": \"Interaction state (start, cancel, progress, completion) is driven entirely through public C# methods/properties that do not require simulated keyboard or Input System device events, so tests can drive it deterministically.\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:31:      \"verification\": \"Code review of the Play Mode test file: confirm every test calls public methods/properties on the interaction/door components and none inject InputSystem device events or legacy Input calls.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:50:      \"requirement\": \"The Editor menu command 'No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene' generates a complete, playable scene at Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity containing a floor, player placeholder, sealed door/doorway, camera, lighting, interaction UI, and a documented damage-test control, with all serialized references auto-wired.\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:51:      \"verification\": \"Manual execution: run the menu command from a clean state, inspect the Hierarchy and Inspector for the listed objects and populated references, then run it again and confirm no console errors and no duplicated hierarchy.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:55:      \"requirement\": \"Running the scene-builder command a second time does not create duplicate GameObjects, UI elements, or scenes, and leaves no null-reference errors in the Console.\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:56:      \"verification\": \"Manual execution: run the menu command twice consecutively from an already-built scene state; diff the Hierarchy object count before and after the second run and confirm it is unchanged.\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:78:    \"A minimal, clearly-labeled debug/testing control (key press or debug UI button) to trigger player damage for demonstrating cancellation\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:79:    \"Idempotent Editor menu command that builds or rebuilds the prototype scene with all references wired\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:80:    \"Play Mode automated tests covering completion, movement cancellation, damage cancellation, and release cancellation\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:81:    \"Assembly definitions for runtime, Editor, and test code as needed to support the above\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:85:    \"com.unity.inputsystem 1.14.0, already present in Packages/manifest.json — required because ProjectSettings/ProjectSettings.asset has activeInputHandler: 1 (Input System Package (New) only); legacy UnityEngine.Input calls will throw InvalidOperationException at runtime and must not be used anywhere in this feature\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:87:    \"Unity Test Framework for Play Mode tests — the implementer must verify its presence (it is a built-in Editor-only test package, not a new external dependency) before relying on it, and must flag rather than silently skip tests if unavailable\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:92:  \"objective\": \"Implement a small, testable Unity 6000.1.8f1 prototype of the core sealed-door interaction from the No Safe Circle GDD: a player must hold an interact input at a sealed door for five uninterrupted seconds (configurable) to open it, with the attempt cancelled deterministically by releasing input, moving, or taking damage, and with visible in-range prompt and progress feedback — built via a repeatable Editor scene-builder command, verified by Play Mode tests, and contained entirely under Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/.\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:106:    \"Runtime generative AI\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:109:    \"Direct hand-editing of .unity scene YAML\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:130:      \"purpose\": \"Minimal damage component exposing a public TakeDamage method, used by the debug damage control and by Play Mode tests to trigger interaction cancellation\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:139:      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:140:      \"purpose\": \"Editor menu command 'No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene' that idempotently generates DoorPrototype.unity with all required objects, UI, and wired serialized references\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:144:      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:145:      \"purpose\": \"Generated prototype scene, produced only by the scene-builder command and never hand-authored\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:149:      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:150:      \"purpose\": \"Play Mode tests covering completion, movement cancellation, damage cancellation, and interaction-release cancellation\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:155:      \"purpose\": \"Assembly definition for the runtime gameplay scripts\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:160:      \"purpose\": \"Assembly definition for the Editor scene-builder script\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:164:      \"path\": \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.asmdef\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:165:      \"purpose\": \"Assembly definition for the Play Mode test assembly, referencing the runtime assembly and the Unity Test Framework\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:175:      \"purpose\": \"Implementer's record of files changed, design decisions, scene-generation instructions, test instructions, known limitations, and confirmation that excluded systems were not implemented\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:180:    \"activeInputHandler is set to 1 (Input System Package (New) only) in ProjectSettings/ProjectSettings.asset; any use of legacy UnityEngine.Input by the implementer will throw InvalidOperationException at runtime — the implementer must use UnityEngine.InputSystem (e.g., Keyboard.current) exclusively\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:181:    \"Scene-builder idempotency is nontrivial: a naive re-run could duplicate GameObjects, UI canvases, or EventSystems unless the builder explicitly finds-and-replaces known objects by name before creating new ones\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:183:    \"The debug damage-test control must be clearly labeled as a testing/demo affordance so it is not mistaken for a combat system, which would blur the boundary with out-of-scope enemy/combat content\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:184:    \"The five-second timer must be measured via accumulated Time.deltaTime (not frame counts) so Play Mode tests that advance simulated time remain deterministic and frame-rate independent\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:185:    \"Presence of the Unity Test Framework package is unconfirmed in the provided manifest excerpt; if it is missing, Play Mode tests cannot compile until it is enabled, and the implementer must flag this rather than silently omitting tests\",\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:186:    \"Scene generation and final visual/interaction correctness are Editor-only concerns that cannot be verified by this planning agent; per CLAUDE.md, human inspection and Play Mode execution remain required before the feature is considered complete\"\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:188:  \"test_cases\": [\r\nAgentCrew/outputs/feature_contract.json:193:      \"expected\": \"DoorInteractable.Progress reaches 1.0 and DoorInteractable.IsOpen becomes true; the doorway no longer blocks a test object moved through it.\"\r\n# Assignment 6 — Build a GER Pipeline for Your Capstone\r\n\r\n**Student:** Vincent Liguori  \r\n**Game:** No Safe Circle  \r\n**Assignment:** Assignment 6 — Build a GER Pipeline for Your Capstone\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\nFor Assignment 6, I built a GER pipeline that generates, evaluates, refines, and, if necessary, escalates Unity implementation work for **No Safe Circle**.\r\n\r\nRather than creating new agents from scratch, I reused systems from earlier assignments:\r\n\r\n- **Assignment 5** selects what feature should be built next.\r\n- The **Assignment 5 implementation agent** acts as both the **Generator** and the **Refiner**.\r\n- The **Assignment 3 validation infrastructure** acts as the **Evaluator**.\r\n- Assignment 6 adds the **GER orchestration**, a game-specific evaluator contract, runtime-feedback handling, and a **Circuit Breaker**.\r\n\r\nThis made Assignment 6 a continuation of the earlier agent work instead of a separate demo.\r\n\r\n## What Content Does the Pipeline Generate?\r\n\r\nThe pipeline generates **Unity implementation code for missing No Safe Circle features**.\r\n\r\nAssignment 5 had already analyzed the current game against the GDD and produced a list of candidate goals. Instead of regenerating that expensive analysis, I added a lightweight reselection step that reused the saved Assignment 5 artifact and excluded the already-completed Mana goal.\r\n\r\nThe next selected goal was:\r\n\r\n> **Fixed Isometric Camera and Projection Setup**\r\n\r\nThe goal was to replace the disposable perspective test camera with the fixed orthographic isometric presentation required by the GDD.\r\n\r\n## GDD Rule Enforced by the Evaluator\r\n\r\nThe Assignment 6 Evaluator enforces a specific No Safe Circle GDD rule rather than performing only generic code validation.\r\n\r\nFor this run, the camera implementation had to satisfy these requirements:\r\n\r\n1. The gameplay Main Camera uses **orthographic projection**.\r\n2. The game maintains a **consistent isometric viewing orientation**.\r\n3. The player cannot freely rotate the world view.\r\n4. The implementation stays inside the selected camera/projection task instead of expanding into unrelated systems such as Tilemaps, spells, enemies, or sprite sorting.\r\n\r\nA script compiling successfully was not enough to pass.\r\n\r\n## GER Architecture\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAssignment 5 Goal Selection\r\n        ↓\r\nnext_goal_selection.json\r\n        ↓\r\nAssignment 5 Implementation Agent\r\n        ↓\r\nGENERATOR\r\n        ↓\r\nAssignment 3 Validation Infrastructure\r\n        ↓\r\nEVALUATOR\r\n      /       \\\r\n   PASS       FAIL\r\n    ↓           ↓\r\nUnity Test   Assignment 5 Implementation Agent\r\n                ↓\r\n             REFINER\r\n                ↓\r\n             EVALUATOR\r\n                ↓\r\n        repeated failures\r\n                ↓\r\n         CIRCUIT BREAKER\r\n                ↓\r\n           Human Review\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe Circuit Breaker allows a maximum of **three refinement attempts**. If the system cannot self-correct within that limit, the task is escalated for human review.\r\n\r\n## What Happened During the Run\r\n\r\n### Implementation Pass 1 — Generator\r\n\r\nThe Assignment 5 implementation agent generated the first camera implementation.\r\n\r\nIt modified:\r\n\r\n- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`\r\n- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs`\r\n\r\nThe implementation changed the camera to orthographic and added tests.\r\n\r\n### Evaluation Pass 1 — Static Pass\r\n\r\nThe Evaluator found that the implementation satisfied all four static acceptance criteria:\r\n\r\n- orthographic projection,\r\n- fixed isometric presentation,\r\n- no free camera rotation,\r\n- correct scope.\r\n\r\nHowever, the checked-in Unity scene was still stale, so I rebuilt the Door Prototype scene in Unity and tested the result manually.\r\n\r\n### Unity Runtime Test 1 — Failure\r\n\r\nThe camera was orthographic, but the game was not usable:\r\n\r\n- the camera faced the wrong direction;\r\n- the camera did not follow the player;\r\n- the static evaluator had interpreted “fixed camera” too narrowly.\r\n\r\nI recorded this as:\r\n\r\n`Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback000.json`\r\n\r\nThe pipeline was updated so human Unity runtime failures could re-enter the GER loop as evaluation feedback.\r\n\r\n### Implementation Pass 2 — Refiner\r\n\r\nThe Assignment 5 implementation agent was reused as the Refiner.\r\n\r\nIt created:\r\n\r\n- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/IsometricCameraFollow.cs`\r\n\r\nand modified the scene builder and camera tests.\r\n\r\nThe camera could now translate with the player while preserving a fixed isometric orientation.\r\n\r\n### Evaluation Pass 2 — Static Pass\r\n\r\nThe Evaluator again passed the implementation statically.\r\n\r\nI rebuilt and tested the scene in Unity again.\r\n\r\n### Unity Runtime Test 2 — Failure\r\n\r\nThe camera still did not frame the game correctly. The player-follow behavior existed, but the viewing side and framing were wrong and the starting door was no longer properly visible.\r\n\r\nI recorded this separately as:\r\n\r\n`Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback001.json`\r\n\r\nRuntime feedback files are preserved as an append-only history:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nruntime_feedback000.json\r\nruntime_feedback001.json\r\nruntime_feedback002.json\r\n...\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Implementation Pass 3 — Refiner Made No Progress\r\n\r\nThe Refiner reported `implemented`, but it made **no observed changes under `Assets/`**.\r\n\r\nThis exposed a weakness in the original Assignment 5 implementation contract: Assignment 5 expected an `implemented` result to always include modified files and would crash otherwise.\r\n\r\nFor Assignment 6, I changed that behavior. A no-op refinement is now treated as a **failed repair attempt**, not a pipeline error.\r\n\r\nThe GER progress guard detected the no-op and forced the loop to continue instead of accepting a false success.\r\n\r\n### Implementation Pass 4 — Final Refinement\r\n\r\nThe final allowed refinement modified the scene builder and its tests again. The Evaluator verified the current camera implementation and the additional regression safeguards.\r\n\r\nThe implementation now used:\r\n\r\n- orthographic projection,\r\n- a consistent isometric orientation,\r\n- camera translation/follow without free rotation,\r\n- stable camera-to-player offset behavior,\r\n- tests for camera framing and fixed rotation,\r\n- a regression check for invalid/null follow targets.\r\n\r\nThe Evaluator returned PASS.\r\n\r\nAt this point, all **three allowed refinements** had been used. If the feature had failed again, the Circuit Breaker would have escalated the task to human review.\r\n\r\n## Final Unity Validation\r\n\r\nAfter the final GER pass, I rebuilt the Door Prototype scene in Unity and tested the result manually.\r\n\r\nThe camera now behaves as intended: it uses a **Diablo / Ultima Online-style orthographic isometric presentation**, follows the player appropriately, and does not allow free camera rotation.\r\n\r\nI then ran the relevant Unity automated tests:\r\n\r\n- the full `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests` EditMode suite;\r\n- the existing `DoorInteractionPlayModeTests` PlayMode suite.\r\n\r\n**All tests passed.**\r\n\r\n## Did the Pipeline Catch Something I Would Have Missed?\r\n\r\nYes.\r\n\r\nThe most useful result of this assignment was discovering that a static evaluator can prove that code satisfies a written requirement while the actual game can still be broken.\r\n\r\nThe first camera implementation passed static evaluation because it was orthographic and used a fixed isometric rotation. In Unity, however, it faced the wrong direction and did not follow the player.\r\n\r\nThe second implementation also passed static evaluation but still framed gameplay incorrectly.\r\n\r\nHuman Play Mode testing exposed those failures. The failures were converted into structured runtime feedback and fed back into GER so the Refiner could continue working on the same selected goal.\r\n\r\nThe pipeline also caught a different failure during Pass 3: the Refiner made no code changes even though unresolved runtime feedback existed. A GER progress guard rejected that no-op repair and counted it toward the Circuit Breaker instead of allowing the pipeline to fail silently.\r\n\r\n## Important Output Artifacts\r\n\r\nThe Assignment 6 run preserves the complete history of the experiment under:\r\n\r\n`Assignment6GER/outputs/`\r\n\r\nImportant artifacts include:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nger_contract.json\r\n\r\nimplementation_pass_1.json\r\nimplementation_pass_2.json\r\nimplementation_pass_3.json\r\nimplementation_pass_4.json\r\n\r\nevaluation_pass_1.json\r\nevaluation_pass_2.json\r\nevaluation_pass_3.json\r\nevaluation_pass_4.json\r\n\r\nruntime_feedback000.json\r\nruntime_feedback001.json\r\n\r\nfinal_result.json\r\nfinal_result_pass_*.json\r\n\r\nevaluator_agent_log_pass_*.json\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis history makes it possible to reconstruct what each agent did, what failed, what feedback was provided, and how the implementation changed.\r\n\r\n## Final Result\r\n\r\nThe GER pipeline successfully demonstrated all four required behaviors:\r\n\r\n- **Generator:** produced Unity implementation code for the selected No Safe Circle feature.\r\n- **Evaluator:** checked the implementation against a specific rule from the No Safe Circle GDD.\r\n- **Refiner:** repeatedly used evaluator and Unity runtime feedback to repair the implementation.\r\n- **Circuit Breaker:** bounded the loop to three refinement attempts and would have escalated to human review if the final attempt failed.\r\n\r\nThe selected camera feature ultimately passed both the GER Evaluator and real Unity validation.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"for f in Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback000.json Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback001.json Assignment6GER/outputs/implementation_pass_3.json Assignment6GER/outputs/final_result.json; do printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,260p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done; rg -n '\"'no.?op|progress|refinement|Assets|runtime_feedback|max_refinements|circuit' Assignment6GER/ger_pipeline.py | sed -n '1,320p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"for f in Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback000.json Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback001.json Assignment6GER/outputs/implementation_pass_3.json Assignment6GER/outputs/final_result.json; do printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,260p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done; rg -n '\"'no.?op|progress|refinement|Assets|runtime_feedback|max_refinements|circuit' Assignment6GER/ger_pipeline.py | sed -n '1,320p'\"","aggregated_output":"Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback000.json\n{\r\n  \"status\": \"needs_changes\",\r\n  \"source\": \"human_unity_runtime_validation\",\r\n  \"summary\": \"The orthographic camera implementation passed static evaluation but failed when tested in Unity: the view points in the wrong direction and the stationary camera makes gameplay unusable as the player moves.\",\r\n  \"developer_interpretation\": \"For this implementation, preserve orthographic projection, a consistent isometric viewing orientation, and no free player-controlled rotation. Do not require the camera to stay at one absolute world-space position if translation/follow behavior is needed to keep gameplay visible.\",\r\n  \"blocking_issues\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"issue\": \"The rebuilt orthographic camera faces the wrong direction.\",\r\n      \"required_fix\": \"Correct the isometric viewing direction/framing while preserving orthographic projection and a consistent isometric orientation.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"issue\": \"The camera remains stationary while the player moves, causing the player/gameplay area to leave the useful view and crippling gameplay.\",\r\n      \"required_fix\": \"Keep the player/gameplay area visible through camera translation or follow behavior while preserving the fixed isometric orientation and preventing free player-controlled camera rotation.\"\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\nAssignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback001.json\n{\r\n  \"status\": \"needs_changes\",\r\n  \"source\": \"human_unity_runtime_validation\",\r\n  \"summary\": \"The second camera implementation still fails in Unity. The camera follows the player, but its viewing side/framing is wrong and the starting door is no longer properly visible.\",\r\n  \"developer_interpretation\": \"The camera should remain a separate Main Camera with a target reference to the player. It should follow the player by maintaining a stable world-space isometric offset rather than continually deriving its position so that camera.forward points directly at the player. The fixed offset and viewing orientation must frame the player and the gameplay space ahead, including the starting door.\",\r\n  \"blocking_issues\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"issue\": \"The camera is positioned on the wrong side of the player and no longer frames the starting door correctly.\",\r\n      \"required_fix\": \"Choose the correct stable isometric world-space offset/orientation so that the player and the starting door/gameplay space are visible from the intended side.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"issue\": \"The follow implementation derives camera position from camera rotation so that camera.forward points directly at the player. This satisfies the static direction test but produces incorrect gameplay framing.\",\r\n      \"required_fix\": \"Use a stable world-space follow offset from the player. Translate that offset with the player while keeping orthographic projection and isometric rotation unchanged.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"issue\": \"The evaluator test that verifies camera.forward points at the player is insufficient to establish correct gameplay framing.\",\r\n      \"required_fix\": \"Replace or supplement that test with checks for a stable player-to-camera offset and fixed rotation. Runtime framing of the player and door must still be confirmed in Unity.\"\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\nAssignment6GER/outputs/implementation_pass_3.json\n{\r\n  \"status\": \"implemented\",\r\n  \"selected_goal_name\": \"Fixed Isometric Camera and Projection Setup\",\r\n  \"selected_goal_description\": \"Replace the disposable perspective test camera with a fixed orthographic isometric camera configuration matching the GDD's Diablo-1/Ultima-Online-inspired fixed 2.5D isometric presentation.\",\r\n  \"implementation_summary\": \"This is the pass-3 refine of the isometric camera, correcting the two blocking issues from the human Unity runtime evaluation of pass 2 (which derived camera position from camera.forward, producing correct-looking static direction but wrong gameplay framing). The fix: BuildCamera() now sets rotation to a plain constant (Euler 30,-45,0) and position to targetPosition + a plain constant world-space offset (10,10,-10) - two independent, hand-verified values rather than one being derived from the other. I hand-verified with the exact rotation/offset/positions used by the scene builder that this places the player at viewport (0.5, ~0.40) and the door at roughly (0.6-0.68, ~0.44), both comfortably inside frame and in front of the camera (positive viewport z) - correcting the 'wrong side / door not visible' defect. IsometricCameraFollow (unchanged from this file's existing correct design) captures that offset once via Initialize() and re-applies target.position + offset every LateUpdate, translating the camera without ever touching its rotation, satisfying the evaluator's 'stable world-space offset, not rotation-derived' requirement. The old Build_MainCamera_ForwardDirectionPointsAtPlayer test (explicitly called out by the evaluator as an insufficient check) has been replaced by Build_MainCamera_FramesPlayerAndStartingDoorInView, which asserts both the player and the starting door land inside the camera viewport at scene start, plus two tests asserting the follow component preserves a stable offset and never modifies rotation while tracking the player.\",\r\n  \"files_created\": [\r\n    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/IsometricCameraFollow.cs\",\r\n    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/IsometricCameraFollow.cs.meta\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"files_modified\": [\r\n    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\n    \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"tests_added\": [\r\n    \"Build_MainCamera_FramesPlayerAndStartingDoorInView - asserts the player and the starting door both land inside the camera viewport (0.1-0.9 x/y, positive z) at scene start, replacing the evaluator-flagged forward-points-at-player check with an actual framing check\",\r\n    \"Build_MainCamera_HasFollowComponentTargetingPlayer - asserts IsometricCameraFollow is attached to Main Camera with its private target field wired to Player\",\r\n    \"Build_MainCamera_TranslatesWithPlayerButRotationStaysFixed - moves the player, invokes the follow component's LateUpdate via reflection, and asserts the camera keeps the same relative world-space offset while its rotation is byte-for-byte unchanged\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"validations\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"Manual math verification of camera framing (player + door in viewport)\",\r\n      \"status\": \"passed\",\r\n      \"details\": \"Computed camera-space basis vectors for rotation Euler(30,-45,0) by hand and projected both the player position (0,1,-4) and door position (0,0,0) against camera position (10,11,-14). Player lands at viewport (~0.5, ~0.40); door lands at roughly (0.60-0.68 depending on aspect, ~0.44). Both are within the test's required 0.1-0.9 x/y bounds with positive viewport z (in front of camera), confirming the reported wrong-side/door-not-visible defect is fixed.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"Targeted diff review scoped to camera-related files\",\r\n      \"status\": \"passed\",\r\n      \"details\": \"git diff --ignore-space-at-eol --stat confirmed only DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs, DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs, and the (stale, human-regenerated-required) DoorPrototype.unity scene have real semantic changes; all other modified files in the directory are pre-existing line-ending-only noise unrelated to this goal.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"Reference/consistency grep for removed and current API usage\",\r\n      \"status\": \"passed\",\r\n      \"details\": \"Confirmed no leftover references to a parameterless BuildCamera() or to any rotation-derived position formula; the single BuildCamera(Transform) call site matches its definition; IsometricCameraOffset constant is used consistently in exactly the two expected places.\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"Brace/paren balance check on edited source file\",\r\n      \"status\": \"passed\",\r\n      \"details\": \"Programmatic count confirmed matching brace and parenthesis counts in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs (no truncation/syntax corruption).\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"Unity compile / Test Runner execution\",\r\n      \"status\": \"not_available\",\r\n      \"details\": \"No Unity executable or UnityHub found in this sandboxed environment, so the scene builder could not be run to regenerate DoorPrototype.unity and the EditMode Test Runner could not execute the new/updated tests.\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"unity_run_status\": \"not_available\",\r\n  \"manual_unity_validation\": [\r\n    \"Open the project in Unity 6000.1.8f1 and run menu 'No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene' to regenerate DoorPrototype.unity - the checked-in scene still reflects an older camera rotation and must be rebuilt from the corrected source.\",\r\n    \"Enter Play Mode: confirm the Main Camera view shows the player, floor, door, and walls centered and comfortably framed at scene start (not looking off-scene or missing the door).\",\r\n    \"Move the player with WASD and confirm the camera translates to keep the same relative framing, with no rotation/tilt change as it follows.\",\r\n    \"Run Window > General > Test Runner > EditMode and confirm all tests in NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor pass, especially Build_MainCamera_FramesPlayerAndStartingDoorInView, Build_MainCamera_HasFollowComponentTargetingPlayer, and Build_MainCamera_TranslatesWithPlayerButRotationStaysFixed.\",\r\n    \"Optionally run the existing PlayMode tests to confirm no regressions from the BuildCamera call-order change (Camera now built after Player).\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"requirements_satisfied\": [\r\n    \"Camera remains orthographic with a fixed 30/-45 dimetric isometric rotation that is never modified after scene build - no free/player-controlled rotation.\",\r\n    \"Camera position uses a stable, independently-verified world-space offset from the player rather than being derived from the camera's own rotation, per the evaluator's required_fix.\",\r\n    \"Player and starting door are both verified (by hand-computed viewport projection) to be comfortably inside the camera's frame at scene start, correcting the 'wrong side / door not visible' defect.\",\r\n    \"The camera follows the player each frame by translating with a fixed offset while rotation stays byte-for-byte constant, verified by a dedicated test that moves the player and invokes LateUpdate directly.\",\r\n    \"The insufficient forward-points-at-player test was replaced with a viewport-framing test covering both the player and the door, per the evaluator's third blocking issue.\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"remaining_work\": [\r\n    \"A human with Unity must run the 'Build Door Prototype Scene' menu command to regenerate the checked-in DoorPrototype.unity scene file, since the currently checked-in scene still serializes the earlier (30,45,0) rotation and predates this fix - scene YAML cannot be hand-edited per project rules.\",\r\n    \"Human/Unity execution of the EditMode test suite to confirm the camera framing and follow tests actually pass at runtime, and the PlayMode suite to confirm no regressions from the BuildCamera call-order change.\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"notes\": \"On inspection, this refine pass's code changes (IsometricCameraFollow.cs, the corrected BuildCamera, and the replacement tests) were already present in the working tree, matching exactly what the evaluator's blocking issues required. I did not find them already validated against the actual required offset/rotation combination, so I independently hand-verified the vector/viewport math for the specific values in the source (offset (10,10,-10), rotation Euler(30,-45,0), player at (0,1,-4), door at (0,0,0)) to confirm the framing is actually correct this time, rather than trusting the code's own comments. Unity was not available in this sandboxed environment, so no compile or Test Runner execution occurred; this is reported honestly as unity_run_status = not_available. The checked-in .unity scene file is stale (still reflects the old (30,45,0) rotation) and must be regenerated by a human via the Editor menu command before this fix is visible in-game.\",\r\n  \"assignment6_ger_role\": \"refiner\",\r\n  \"assignment6_pass_number\": 3,\r\n  \"observed_assets_delta\": {\r\n    \"created\": [],\r\n    \"modified\": [],\r\n    \"deleted\": []\r\n  },\r\n  \"assignment6_refinement_noop\": true\r\n}\r\nAssignment6GER/outputs/final_result.json\n{\r\n  \"game\": \"No Safe Circle\",\r\n  \"selected_goal\": {\r\n    \"name\": \"Fixed Isometric Camera and Projection Setup\",\r\n    \"description\": \"Replace the disposable perspective test camera with a fixed orthographic isometric camera configuration matching the GDD's Diablo-1/Ultima-Online-inspired fixed 2.5D isometric presentation.\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"gdd_rule_id\": \"gdd-fixed-orthographic-isometric-camera\",\r\n  \"status\": \"accepted_static\",\r\n  \"reason\": \"The implementation passed the Assignment 6 static GDD-specific Evaluator. If this run followed human runtime feedback, the developer must now test the refined result again in Unity.\",\r\n  \"implementation_passes\": 4,\r\n  \"refinements_used\": 3,\r\n  \"circuit_breaker\": {\r\n    \"max_refinements\": 3,\r\n    \"refinements_used\": 3,\r\n    \"tripped\": true\r\n  },\r\n  \"latest_implementation\": {\r\n    \"status\": \"implemented\",\r\n    \"selected_goal_name\": \"Fixed Isometric Camera and Projection Setup\",\r\n    \"selected_goal_description\": \"Replace the disposable perspective test camera with a fixed orthographic isometric camera configuration matching the GDD's Diablo-1/Ultima-Online-inspired fixed 2.5D isometric presentation.\",\r\n    \"implementation_summary\": \"Inspected the working tree and confirmed the camera implementation from the prior refinement pass (orthographic projection, fixed 30/-45/0 rotation, constant world-space follow offset via IsometricCameraFollow, viewport-framing tests) is present and already satisfies all four evaluator acceptance criteria. Since the pipeline's guardrail specifically failed pass 3 for making no observed Assets/ changes, this pass made a concrete, in-scope repair addressing the evaluator's own compile_risks note about the fragile BuildPlayer-before-BuildCamera ordering dependency: (1) added an explanatory comment at the Build() call site documenting why that order matters, (2) changed BuildCamera to emit a Debug.LogWarning if ever invoked with a null follow target instead of silently placing the camera at the world origin (which would silently violate the 'consistent isometric presentation' criterion if the ordering were ever broken by a future edit), and (3) added a new EditMode regression test that invokes BuildCamera directly with a null target and asserts the warning fires via LogAssert.Expect, locking this defensive behavior in. No camera projection, rotation, offset math, or scope was changed - the fix hardens the existing correct implementation against future regressions rather than altering its already-passing behavior.\",\r\n    \"files_created\": [],\r\n    \"files_modified\": [\r\n      \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\n      \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"tests_added\": [\r\n      \"BuildCamera_NullFollowTarget_LogsWarningInsteadOfSilentlyMisframing — invokes the private BuildCamera(Transform) method via reflection with a null target and asserts (via LogAssert.Expect) that a warning is logged, and that a valid orthographic Main Camera is still created.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"validations\": [\r\n      {\r\n        \"name\": \"Manual re-read of edited files\",\r\n        \"status\": \"passed\",\r\n        \"details\": \"Re-read DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs and DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs in full after edits; confirmed the new comment, null-guard warning, using UnityEngine.TestTools import, and new test are syntactically consistent with the existing code style and asmdef references (NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef already references UnityEngine.TestRunner, which provides UnityEngine.TestTools.LogAssert).\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"name\": \"git diff review vs HEAD\",\r\n        \"status\": \"passed\",\r\n        \"details\": \"git diff confirms the working tree's DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs differs substantially from the committed HEAD version (which still has the old perspective camera at (0,8,-8) rotated (45,0,0) with no follow behavior), and my new edits are layered on top of that already-corrected in-scope isometric camera code.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"name\": \"Unity compile / EditMode Test Runner\",\r\n        \"status\": \"not_available\",\r\n        \"details\": \"No Unity executable is available in this container; could not run the Editor or Test Runner.\"\r\n      }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"unity_run_status\": \"not_available\",\r\n    \"manual_unity_validation\": [\r\n      \"Open the project in Unity 6000.1.8f1.\",\r\n      \"Run menu 'No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene' to regenerate the still-stale checked-in DoorPrototype.unity scene from the current builder script.\",\r\n      \"Open Window > General > Test Runner, switch to EditMode, and run all tests in NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor — confirm all DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests pass, including the new BuildCamera_NullFollowTarget_LogsWarningInsteadOfSilentlyMisframing test.\",\r\n      \"Enter Play Mode and visually confirm the Main Camera is a fixed orthographic isometric view that translates with the player but never rotates, with the player and starting door both comfortably framed at scene start.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"requirements_satisfied\": [\r\n      \"Camera remains orthographic with a fixed isometric rotation (30, -45, 0) that is never modified after scene build, matching the GDD's Diablo 1 / Ultima Online-style fixed 2.5D presentation.\",\r\n      \"Camera position is driven by IsometricCameraFollow using a plain constant world-space offset captured once, decoupled from rotation, so no free camera rotation is possible.\",\r\n      \"Addressed the evaluator's compile_risks note about the fragile BuildPlayer-before-BuildCamera build ordering by documenting it and making the failure mode observable (a warning) instead of a silent mis-framing bug.\",\r\n      \"Added a genuine, concrete, in-scope test/code change this pass (satisfying the Assignment 6 refinement-progress guardrail that failed on the prior no-op pass), without altering or re-broadening the already-passing camera behavior.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"remaining_work\": [\r\n      \"A human must regenerate the checked-in DoorPrototype.unity scene via the 'No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene' menu command in a real Unity Editor, since hand-editing scene YAML is disallowed and this static pass cannot run Unity.\",\r\n      \"A human must run the EditMode Test Runner (and ideally Play Mode) in real Unity to confirm the new BuildCamera_NullFollowTarget_LogsWarningInsteadOfSilentlyMisframing test and all existing camera tests actually pass, since no Unity executable was available in this environment to verify compilation or test execution.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"notes\": \"This was a REFINE pass per the selected-goal contract's assignment6_refinement_instruction. The prior pass (pass 3) had already produced correct camera code that passed all four evaluator acceptance criteria, but the pipeline's own guardrail failed that pass specifically because it made no observed changes under Assets/. Rather than re-deriving the already-correct camera math or expanding scope, this pass made a small, targeted, camera-scoped hardening change directly responsive to the evaluator's own compile_risks commentary, so the guardrail's 'concrete progress' requirement is met with real, defensible, in-scope work rather than a cosmetic re-save.\",\r\n    \"assignment6_ger_role\": \"refiner\",\r\n    \"assignment6_pass_number\": 4,\r\n    \"observed_assets_delta\": {\r\n      \"created\": [],\r\n      \"modified\": [\r\n        \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\n        \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs\"\r\n      ],\r\n      \"deleted\": []\r\n    },\r\n    \"assignment6_refinement_noop\": false\r\n  },\r\n  \"latest_evaluation\": {\r\n    \"status\": \"pass\",\r\n    \"summary\": \"Pass-4 refinement of the Fixed Isometric Camera goal is verified against the current code. The camera-position/rotation math that resolves the human runtime_feedback (stable world-space offset (10,10,-10) decoupled from rotation, fixed (30,-45,0) orientation, viewport-framing test replacing the flawed forward-points-at-player test) was already independently re-verified by the prior evaluation pass and remains byte-for-byte unchanged in this pass. This pass's own concrete delta — a build-order comment, a null-follow-target Debug.LogWarning fallback in BuildCamera, and a new regression test (BuildCamera_NullFollowTarget_LogsWarningInsteadOfSilentlyMisframing) — is present in the actual files, matches implementation_result.json's claims exactly, and is camera-scoped only. All four acceptance criteria pass with concrete evidence, no free-rotation code exists anywhere in Assets/, no scope expansion into Tilemap/sprite-sorting/spells/enemies is present, and the GDD's 'fixed isometric, no free player rotation' rule (No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md:221) is preserved while the camera-follow translation stays within the allowed camera slice per the contract's own scope note.\",\r\n    \"criteria_results\": [\r\n      {\r\n        \"id\": \"orthographic_projection\",\r\n        \"status\": \"pass\",\r\n        \"evidence\": \"DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildCamera() sets camera.orthographic = true and camera.orthographicSize = IsometricOrthographicSize (8f) at Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:136-137. IsometricCameraFollow.cs (the only other script that touches the camera GameObject) only ever writes transform.position in LateUpdate (IsometricCameraFollow.cs:25-30) and never touches the Camera component's orthographic flag.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"id\": \"consistent_isometric_presentation\",\r\n        \"status\": \"pass\",\r\n        \"evidence\": \"cameraObject.transform.rotation = Quaternion.Euler(IsometricCameraEulerAngles) with IsometricCameraEulerAngles = (30,-45,0) is set once in BuildCamera() (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:22,139) and never modified afterward; IsometricCameraFollow.LateUpdate only writes position (IsometricCameraFollow.cs:25-30). Position is targetPosition + a plain constant IsometricCameraOffset (10,10,-10) (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:29,149) captured once by IsometricCameraFollow.Initialize and reapplied every frame (IsometricCameraFollow.cs:19-23,29), so position is decoupled from rotation rather than derived from camera.forward, which was the specific human runtime_feedback defect. This offset/rotation combination was independently hand-verified against the player (0,1,-4) and door (0,0,0) positions in the prior evaluation pass, landing both inside the required 0.1-0.9 viewport bounds with positive depth, and is exercised at runtime by the still-present Build_MainCamera_FramesPlayerAndStartingDoorInView test (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:132-156). Neither the offset, rotation constants, nor this test were touched in this pass.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"id\": \"no_free_camera_rotation\",\r\n        \"status\": \"pass\",\r\n        \"evidence\": \"Grepped every script under Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts for Camera/Rotate/mouseX/mouseY/Input.GetAxis references; only IsometricCameraFollow.cs matches, and it exclusively writes transform.position in LateUpdate (IsometricCameraFollow.cs:25-30), never rotation. Build_MainCamera_TranslatesWithPlayerButRotationStaysFixed (DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs:174-197) asserts rotation is byte-for-byte unchanged after the player moves and LateUpdate runs. No input-driven or orbit-style rotation code exists anywhere in the project.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"id\": \"selected_goal_scope\",\r\n        \"status\": \"pass\",\r\n        \"evidence\": \"implementation_result.json's files_modified for this pass are limited to DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs and DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs; direct inspection confirms the only substantive change is a build-order comment (lines 66-69), a null-target Debug.LogWarning fallback inside BuildCamera (lines 141-146), and one new test (BuildCamera_NullFollowTarget_LogsWarningInsteadOfSilentlyMisframing, tests file lines 199-222). No Tilemap authoring, sprite-sorting, spell, or enemy code appears anywhere in the diffed files. The camera-follow translation behavior (IsometricCameraFollow) predates this pass and is explicitly permitted camera scope per ger_contract.json's own verification note.\"\r\n      }\r\n    ],\r\n    \"blocking_issues\": [],\r\n    \"compile_risks\": [\r\n      \"None newly introduced this pass. The new test's LogAssert.Expect call relies on UnityEngine.TestTools, which the test asmdef already references via UnityEngine.TestRunner (NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef); the file's `using UnityEngine.TestTools;` import (line 7) is present and consistent. IsometricCameraFollow (unqualified reference at DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs:151) resolves via C#'s enclosing-namespace lookup from NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Editor up to NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype, the same pattern already used for PlayerMovement/PlayerHealth/etc. in this file, so no new using-directive risk exists.\",\r\n      \"Unverifiable without a real Unity Editor: actual compilation and EditMode Test Runner execution were not run in this environment (correctly disclosed as unity_run_status = not_available).\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"non_blocking_notes\": [\r\n      \"The checked-in DoorPrototype.unity scene is stale relative to the current builder script per the project's no-hand-edited-scene-YAML rule; implementation_result.json correctly discloses this as remaining_work requiring a human to run 'No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene' and the EditMode/Play Mode Test Runner in real Unity.\",\r\n      \"This pass's claim that the pre-existing pass-3 camera math was already correct is consistent with the independent viewport-math verification recorded in evaluation_pass_3.json, so relying on that prior verification here (rather than re-deriving it) is reasonable and not an unsupported claim.\",\r\n      \"No claim of Unity compilation, Play Mode, or Test Runner execution is made anywhere in implementation_result.json; all such items are honestly marked not_available/remaining_work, consistent with the GER instructions.\"\r\n    ],\r\n    \"files_reviewed\": [\r\n      \"Assignment6GER/outputs/ger_contract.json\",\r\n      \"Assignment6GER/outputs/implementation_result.json\",\r\n      \"Assignment6GER/outputs/evaluation_pass_3.json\",\r\n      \"Assignment6GER/outputs/implementation_pass_3.json\",\r\n      \"Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\",\r\n      \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs\",\r\n      \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/IsometricCameraFollow.cs\",\r\n      \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs\",\r\n      \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef\",\r\n      \"Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.asmdef\"\r\n    ]\r\n  },\r\n  \"completed_at_utc\": \"2026-08-19T01:49:04.296412+00:00\",\r\n  \"runtime_feedback_used\": {\r\n    \"status\": \"needs_changes\",\r\n    \"source\": \"human_unity_runtime_validation\",\r\n    \"summary\": \"The second camera implementation still fails in Unity. The camera follows the player, but its viewing side/framing is wrong and the starting door is no longer properly visible.\",\r\n    \"developer_interpretation\": \"The camera should remain a separate Main Camera with a target reference to the player. It should follow the player by maintaining a stable world-space isometric offset rather than continually deriving its position so that camera.forward points directly at the player. The fixed offset and viewing orientation must frame the player and the gameplay space ahead, including the starting door.\",\r\n    \"blocking_issues\": [\r\n      {\r\n        \"issue\": \"The camera is positioned on the wrong side of the player and no longer frames the starting door correctly.\",\r\n        \"required_fix\": \"Choose the correct stable isometric world-space offset/orientation so that the player and the starting door/gameplay space are visible from the intended side.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"issue\": \"The follow implementation derives camera position from camera rotation so that camera.forward points directly at the player. This satisfies the static direction test but produces incorrect gameplay framing.\",\r\n        \"required_fix\": \"Use a stable world-space follow offset from the player. Translate that offset with the player while keeping orthographic projection and isometric rotation unchanged.\"\r\n      },\r\n      {\r\n        \"issue\": \"The evaluator test that verifies camera.forward points at the player is insufficient to establish correct gameplay framing.\",\r\n        \"required_fix\": \"Replace or supplement that test with checks for a stable player-to-camera offset and fixed rotation. Runtime framing of the player and door must still be confirmed in Unity.\"\r\n      }\r\n    ]\r\n  },\r\n  \"runtime_feedback_history\": [\r\n    \"Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback000.json\",\r\n    \"Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedback001.json\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"final_result_history\": [\r\n    \"Assignment6GER/outputs/final_result_pass_002.json\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"evaluator_log_history\": [\r\n    \"Assignment6GER/outputs/evaluator_agent_log_pass_3.json\",\r\n    \"Assignment6GER/outputs/evaluator_agent_log_pass_4.json\"\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n26:from circuit_breaker import CircuitBreaker\r\n41:RUNTIME_FEEDBACK_PREFIX = \"runtime_feedback\"\r\n44:# previous-assignment infrastructure. Assets/ is the implementation workspace.\r\n57:    GER_ROOT / \"circuit_breaker.py\",\r\n91:def validate_runtime_feedback(feedback: dict[str, Any]) -> None:\r\n130:def runtime_feedback_history_paths() -> list[Path]:\r\n131:    pattern = re.compile(r\"^runtime_feedback(\\d{3})\\.json$\")\r\n137:    for path in OUTPUT_ROOT.glob(\"runtime_feedback*.json\"):\r\n145:def persist_runtime_feedback_history(\r\n152:    If the caller already supplied Assignment6GER/outputs/runtime_feedbackNNN.json,\r\n156:    runtime_feedback000.json, runtime_feedback001.json, ...\r\n164:    history_pattern = re.compile(r\"^runtime_feedback(\\d{3})\\.json$\")\r\n177:    existing_history = runtime_feedback_history_paths()\r\n249:    runtime_feedback: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\r\n276:            \"circuit_breaker\": (\r\n282:    if runtime_feedback is not None:\r\n283:        contract[\"runtime_feedback\"] = runtime_feedback\r\n284:        contract[\"runtime_feedback_policy\"] = (\r\n308:                \"assignment6_ger_refinement\": True,\r\n336:        candidate[\"assignment6_refinement_instruction\"] = (\r\n352:    during Assignment 6 refinement.\r\n394:    assets_before = assignment5_impl.snapshot_path(ROOT / \"Assets\")\r\n413:    assets_after = assignment5_impl.snapshot_path(ROOT / \"Assets\")\r\n430:        # During refinement, a no-op is not a Python/orchestration crash. It is\r\n443:    result[\"assignment6_refinement_noop\"] = (\r\n450:    if result[\"assignment6_refinement_noop\"]:\r\n452:            \"WARNING: Refiner made no observed Assets/ changes. \"\r\n463:    print(\"Observed Assets/ delta:\")\r\n599:                \"If that contract contains runtime_feedback, verify that the \"\r\n635:def enforce_refinement_progress(\r\n641:    A refinement triggered by a concrete failed evaluation/runtime observation\r\n642:    cannot be considered repaired when the Refiner changed nothing in Assets/.\r\n646:    no-op consumes a refinement attempt and can eventually trip the Circuit\r\n652:    if implementation.get(\"assignment6_refinement_noop\") is not True:\r\n666:                \"The Refiner made no observed changes under Assets/ even though \"\r\n679:        \"GER refinement-progress guard failed: the Refiner made no observed \"\r\n680:        \"Assets/ changes in response to unresolved feedback.\"\r\n688:        \"name\": \"refinement_must_make_progress\",\r\n691:            \"A no-op refinement cannot resolve a human/runtime or evaluator \"\r\n709:    runtime_feedback: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\r\n718:        \"refinements_used\": breaker.refinements_used,\r\n719:        \"circuit_breaker\": breaker.status(),\r\n725:    if runtime_feedback is not None:\r\n726:        payload[\"runtime_feedback_used\"] = runtime_feedback\r\n727:        history = runtime_feedback_history_paths()\r\n729:            payload[\"runtime_feedback_history\"] = [\r\n766:def previous_refinement_count() -> int:\r\n775:    value = previous.get(\"refinements_used\", 0)\r\n833:    max_refinements: int,\r\n834:    runtime_feedback_path: Path | None,\r\n841:    runtime_feedback: dict[str, Any] | None = None\r\n846:    initial_refinements = 0\r\n848:    if runtime_feedback_path is not None:\r\n849:        runtime_feedback = load_json(runtime_feedback_path)\r\n850:        validate_runtime_feedback(runtime_feedback)\r\n865:        history_path = persist_runtime_feedback_history(\r\n866:            runtime_feedback_path,\r\n867:            runtime_feedback,\r\n875:        initial_refinements = previous_refinement_count()\r\n876:        feedback = runtime_feedback\r\n883:        runtime_feedback,\r\n895:    print(f\"Circuit breaker: {max_refinements} refinements maximum\")\r\n899:        max_refinements=max_refinements,\r\n900:        refinements_used=initial_refinements,\r\n903:    if runtime_feedback is not None:\r\n905:        print(f\"Runtime feedback source: {runtime_feedback['source']}\")\r\n919:                evaluation=runtime_feedback,\r\n922:                    \"Circuit Breaker has no refinement attempts remaining.\"\r\n924:                runtime_feedback=runtime_feedback,\r\n931:        # refinement attempt.\r\n932:        breaker.record_refinement()\r\n956:                runtime_feedback=runtime_feedback,\r\n967:        evaluation = enforce_refinement_progress(\r\n992:                runtime_feedback=runtime_feedback,\r\n997:                f\"with {breaker.refinements_used} refinement(s) used.\"\r\n999:            if runtime_feedback is not None:\r\n1023:                    \"refinement attempts.\"\r\n1025:                runtime_feedback=runtime_feedback,\r\n1029:                f\"Stopped after {breaker.refinements_used} refinement(s).\"\r\n1033:        breaker.record_refinement()\r\n1052:        \"--max-refinements\",\r\n1056:            \"Maximum refinement passes before the circuit breaker \"\r\n1067:            \"Each feedback artifact is preserved as runtime_feedbackNNN.json.\"\r\n1076:    if args.max_refinements < 0:\r\n1077:        print(\"--max-refinements must be >= 0\", file=sys.stderr)\r\n1082:            max_refinements=args.max_refinements,\r\n1083:            runtime_feedback_path=args.runtime_feedback,\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' AgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md && sed -n '90,190p' AgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md && sed -n '1,180p' AgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json && sed -n '1,260p' AgentCrew/README.md 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' AgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md && sed -n '90,190p' AgentCrew/outputs/implementation_summary.md && sed -n '1,180p' AgentCrew/outputs/crew_run_log.json && sed -n '1,260p' AgentCrew/README.md 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"# Human Playtest Feedback — Door Progress Indicator\r\n\r\n## Result\r\n\r\nThe sealed door opens correctly after holding E for five seconds.\r\n\r\n## Defect\r\n\r\nThe visible progress bar appears, but it does not visually update while the interaction is occurring.\r\n\r\n## Expected Behavior\r\n\r\nWhile E is held and the interaction remains uninterrupted, the progress indicator should visibly increase from 0 to 1 over five seconds.\r\n\r\nMovement, damage, or releasing E should reset the indicator to 0.\r\n\r\n## Required Repair\r\n\r\nInspect the generated UI component, scene-builder configuration, and door progress binding.\r\n\r\nFix the underlying generated implementation rather than relying on a manual Inspector adjustment.\r\n\r\nEnsure the scene-builder command produces the correct configuration every time it runs.\r\n\r\nAdd or update automated coverage where practical.\r\n\r\n## Human Retest Requirements\r\n\r\n1. Rebuild the prototype scene.\r\n2. Hold E and confirm the bar visibly fills over five seconds.\r\n3. Release E and confirm it resets.\r\n4. Move and confirm it resets.\r\n5. Trigger damage and confirm it resets.\r\n6. Confirm the door still opens after an uninterrupted five seconds.\r\n7. Run the scene-builder twice and confirm the repair persists.\r\n   top-left corner and reads WASD / Hold E / interruption rule / the labeled\r\n   debug key.\r\n4. Confirm the panel does not overlap the interaction prompt, the progress\r\n   bar, or the debug damage button.\r\n5. Walk into the door's trigger zone, hold **E**, and confirm the progress\r\n   bar still visibly fills over five seconds (the prior progress-indicator\r\n   repair below is unaffected).\r\n6. Release **E** before completion; confirm the bar resets to empty.\r\n7. Hold **E**, move with WASD before completion; confirm the bar resets to\r\n   empty.\r\n8. Hold **E**, press **K** (or click the debug button) before completion;\r\n   confirm the bar resets to empty.\r\n9. Hold **E** uninterrupted for five seconds; confirm the bar fills and the\r\n   door opens and permits passage.\r\n10. Run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene** again; confirm no\r\n    Console errors and exactly one `ControlsHud` panel (no duplicates), then\r\n    repeat steps 3–9.\r\n11. Open **Window → General → Test Runner** and run the **Edit Mode** tests\r\n    in `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor` and the **Play Mode** tests\r\n    in `NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests`; confirm all pass.\r\n\r\n## Targeted Repair — Progress Indicator Not Visually Updating (2026-08-05)\r\n\r\n### Human-Reported Defect\r\n\r\nFrom `AgentCrew/inputs/human_playtest_feedback.md`: the door opens correctly after\r\nholding E for five seconds, but the visible progress bar does not visually\r\nupdate while the interaction is in progress — it appears but never animates.\r\n\r\n### Diagnosed Cause\r\n\r\nThe runtime binding in `DoorInteractionUI.Update()` (`progressFillImage.fillAmount\r\n= door.Progress`) and the underlying `DoorInteractable.Tick`/`Progress` logic were\r\nalready correct. The defect was in scene generation: `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.BuildUI`\r\ncreated the progress-fill `Image` with `type = Image.Type.Filled` and\r\n`fillMethod = Image.FillMethod.Horizontal` but never assigned a `sprite`.\r\nUnity's `Image.OnPopulateMesh` falls back to drawing a full, unfilled solid\r\nrect whenever `sprite == null`, regardless of `type`/`fillMethod`/`fillAmount` —\r\nso the bar always rendered as a static, fully-filled rectangle no matter what\r\n`Progress` reported. Confirmed directly in the previously-generated\r\n`DoorPrototype.unity` (the fill `Image` entry had `m_Sprite: {fileID: 0}` with\r\n`m_Type: 3`, i.e. Filled with no sprite). This is exactly the kind of defect a\r\none-off manual Inspector fix (assigning a sprite by hand) would mask without\r\nsurviving a scene rebuild.\r\n\r\n### Repair\r\n\r\n`Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`:\r\n- The progress indicator is now two nested UI objects: a static \"ProgressFill\"\r\n  background `Image` (sliced, using the built-in `UI/Skin/Background.psd`\r\n  sprite, for a visible track) and a child \"Fill\" `Image` bound to\r\n  `DoorInteractionUI.progressFillImage`.\r\n- The \"Fill\" `Image` is assigned the built-in `UI/Skin/UISprite.psd` sprite\r\n  (via `AssetDatabase.GetBuiltinExtraResource<Sprite>`, an Editor-only,\r\n  engine-provided resource — no external package or imported asset), with\r\n  `type = Filled`, `fillMethod = Horizontal`, `fillOrigin = Left`. With a real\r\n  sprite present, `fillAmount` now actually drives visible fill geometry.\r\n- No runtime script changes were needed — `DoorInteractionUI` and\r\n  `DoorInteractable` were already correct; the fix is entirely in the\r\n  generated scene configuration, so rebuilding the scene is sufficient and no\r\n  manual Inspector step is required or relied upon.\r\n\r\n### Automated Coverage Added\r\n\r\nNew `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Tests/Editor/` Edit Mode test assembly\r\n(`NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Tests.Editor.asmdef`, Editor-platform only,\r\nreferencing the runtime and Editor assemblies) with\r\n`DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests.cs`:\r\n- `Build_ProgressFillImage_HasSpriteAssignedSoFillAmountIsVisible` — runs the\r\n  scene builder and asserts the generated `Canvas/ProgressFill/Fill` `Image`\r\n  has a non-null `sprite`, `Image.Type.Filled`, and\r\n  `Image.FillMethod.Horizontal`. This regresses directly on the defect: it\r\n  would have failed against the pre-repair builder and fails again if the\r\n  sprite assignment is ever dropped.\r\n- `Build_RunTwice_DoesNotDuplicateProgressFillHierarchy` — runs the builder\r\n  twice and asserts exactly one `ProgressFill` object exists under `Canvas`\r\n  afterward, covering AC-11 idempotency for this specific UI element.\r\n\r\nThe existing five Play Mode tests in\r\n`Tests/DoorInteractionPlayModeTests.cs` (completion, half-duration progress,\r\nrelease/movement/damage cancellation) were not modified — the underlying\r\n`DoorInteractable`/`PlayerInteractionController` logic they exercise was\r\nalready correct and is unaffected by this repair.\r\n\r\n### Exact Human Retest Steps\r\n\r\n1. Open the project in Unity `6000.1.8f1` and let scripts finish compiling.\r\n2. Run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene**.\r\n3. Enter Play Mode. Walk into the door's trigger zone and hold **E**; confirm\r\n   the progress bar visibly fills from empty to full over five seconds.\r\n4. Release **E** before completion; confirm the bar resets to empty.\r\n5. Hold **E**, move with WASD before completion; confirm the bar resets to\r\n   empty.\r\n6. Hold **E**, click the labeled **DEBUG: Take Damage (K)** button (or press\r\n   **K**) before completion; confirm the bar resets to empty.\r\n7. Hold **E** uninterrupted for the full five seconds; confirm the bar reaches\r\n   full and the door opens and permits passage.\r\n8. Exit Play Mode and run **No Safe Circle → Build Door Prototype Scene**\r\n   again; confirm no Console errors, no duplicated Hierarchy objects, and\r\n   repeat steps 3–7 to confirm the repair persists across rebuilds.\r\n9. Open **Window → General → Test Runner**: run the **Edit Mode** tests in\r\n{\r\n  \"game\": \"No Safe Circle\",\r\n  \"feature\": \"Sealed Door Prototype\",\r\n  \"orchestration\": \"Four independent Claude Code invocations with artifact handoffs\",\r\n  \"model\": \"sonnet\",\r\n  \"started_at_utc\": \"2026-08-04T23:49:25.367500+00:00\",\r\n  \"finished_at_utc\": \"2026-08-05T00:02:51.221102+00:00\",\r\n  \"status\": \"success\",\r\n  \"agents\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"role\": \"Feature Planning Agent\",\r\n      \"prompt\": \"AgentCrew/prompts/01_planner.md\",\r\n      \"started_at_utc\": \"2026-08-04T23:49:25.376832+00:00\",\r\n      \"finished_at_utc\": \"2026-08-04T23:50:42.498208+00:00\",\r\n      \"status\": \"completed\",\r\n      \"duration_seconds\": 77.12,\r\n      \"session_id\": \"22dca340-070c-4798-be79-2cf0f7e03f96\",\r\n      \"num_turns\": 16,\r\n      \"result_excerpt\": \"{\\\"acceptance_criteria\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-1\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Holding the interact input while stationary, in range, and undamaged for the full configured duration (default 5.0s) opens the door and permits crossing the doorway.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: drive the interaction start method on the interaction component, advance simulated time by 5+ seconds without cancelling, assert IsOpen becomes true and progress reaches 1.0.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-2\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Door opening progress is exposed as a public normalized float (0 to 1) that increases monotonically while an uninterrupted interaction is held.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance time to 2 of 5 seconds, assert progress is approximately 0.4 within tolerance of Â±0.05.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-3\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Releasing the interact input before completion cancels the attempt and resets progress to exactly 0.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, call the public release/end method, assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-4\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Player movement during an active, incomplete interaction cancels the attempt and resets progress to exactly 0.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, invoke the public movement-cancellation entry point directly (not via WASD polling), assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-5\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Player damage during an active, incomplete interaction cancels the attempt and resets progress to exactly 0.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Play Mode test: begin interaction, advance partial time, call the health component's public TakeDamage method, assert progress equals 0 and IsOpen is false.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"AC-6\\\",\\\"requirement\\\":\\\"Interaction state (start, cancel, progress, completion) is driven entirely through public C# methods/properties that do not require simulated keyboard or Input System device events, so tests can drive it deterministically.\\\",\\\"verification\\\":\\\"Code review of the Play Mode test fi\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"role\": \"Door and Interaction Agent\",\r\n      \"prompt\": \"AgentCrew/prompts/02_implementer.md\",\r\n      \"started_at_utc\": \"2026-08-04T23:50:42.518756+00:00\",\r\n      \"finished_at_utc\": \"2026-08-04T23:58:20.793439+00:00\",\r\n      \"status\": \"completed\",\r\n      \"duration_seconds\": 458.29,\r\n      \"session_id\": \"9355a15a-4dc4-49a9-9b5c-77eebf67e5b0\",\r\n      \"num_turns\": 28,\r\n      \"result_excerpt\": \"All required files (except the Editor-generated `.unity` scene, which per contract must be produced by running the menu command in Unity) are in place.\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nImplemented the Sealed Door Prototype under `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/`:\\n\\n- **Runtime**: `DoorInteractable` (progress/duration/open state + public `Tick()` for deterministic testing), `PlayerInteractionController` (input-independent `BeginInteraction`/`EndInteraction`/`OnPlayerMoved`/range notifications), `PlayerMovement` (WASD via new Input System), `PlayerHealth` (`TakeDamage` + event), `DoorInteractionUI` (prompt/progress binding), `DebugDamageControl` (labeled testing affordance, key + button).\\n- **Editor**: `DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs` implementing `No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene`, idempotently rebuilding the full scene